\section{Introduction}

% What's the problem?
We receive emails from students asking why they haven't gotten their grades 
reported yet.
% Why is it a problem? Research gap left by other approaches?
Since all grades are reported automatically, this means that there is something 
they haven't completed.
% Why is it important? Why care?
We still have to answer these emails, which takes time.
So we want to reduce the time it takes to answer these emails.

% What's the approach? How to solve the problem?
Our approach is to write utilities that parse the email and generate the needed 
commands to find the students results.
We need to look these up to answer the question and it's good to send these to 
the student so that they see.
% What's the findings? How was it evaluated, what are the results, limitations, 
% what remains to be done?

In this era, a first thought might be that this would require the use of an LLM 
to read the email and generate the commands to fetch the results.
(Or that the LLM simply calls those commands directly.)
That would be a complete waste of energy since we can do something that is more 
efficient and more deterministic (more sustainable and less error prone).

This is the literate source code of the [[<<[[edumail.sh]]>>]] bash script.
The idea is that it's a command that works like Git with different subcommands.
Then we simply call it when we are processing a student email.
We'll use a simple construction to achieve this.

\subsection{Structural overview}

We'll start with a [[<<[[edumail.sh]]>>]] script that contains the main 
function.
This function will parse the first argument as the subcommand to call.
Then it will call that subcommand function with the rest of the arguments.
\begin{minted}{bash}
edumail results email.txt
\end{minted}
This calls the [[results]] function with the argument [[email.txt]].

We'll execute the [[main]] function if the script is not sourced\footnote{%
  Sourced: included in another script as a library.%
}.
This way, we can source the script and use the functions directly in the 
current shell.
So instead of \mintinline{bash}{edumail results email.txt}, we can do
\begin{minted}{bash}
source edumail.sh
results email.txt
\end{minted}

We'll use a rather standard structure for the script.
<<[[edumail.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<variables>>
<<functions>>

main() {
  <<parse which subcommand should be called and call its function>>
}

# call main if not sourced
# https://stackoverflow.com/a/28776166/1305099
(return 0 2>/dev/null) || main "$@"
@

\subsection{Invoke right subcommand function}

The first argument ([[$1]]) should be the subcommand.
This should be the same as the name of its function.
This way we can simply shift the arguments and call that function,
then it can parse the rest of the arguments as it sees fit.
However, an easier way in this case is to simply evaluate [["$@"]],
this would have the same effect as shifting the arguments and calling the 
function.

If no subcommand is given, we print a usage message.
This should include a list of all subcommands.
It should look like this:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", ""], capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

For this we use a trick of using the side effects of boolean operators:
[[bash]] only evaluates what is needed---for an \enquote{and} operator 
([[&&]]) we don't evaluate the second if the first is false (can't get two true 
values if the first is false), for the \enquote{or} operator ([[||]]) we don't 
evaluate the second if the first is true (we already have a true value, so the 
second can't change the outcome).
<<parse which subcommand should be called and call its function>>=
[[ "$1" = "" ]] \
  && <<echo help text>> \
  || "$@"
<<echo help text>>=
echo -e "Usage: $0 [function] args\nFunctions:\n<<list all functions>>"
@

To get a list of all the functions, we simply grep them out of the script 
itself, given by [[$0]].
We skip the [[main]] function, since that one it not interesting.
<<list all functions>>=
$(sed -En 's/^(\w+)\(\) \{/  \1/p' $0 \
  | grep -Pv '^\s*main$')
@

\section{The results subcommands}

We want a set of subcommands that can be used to get a student's results from 
the course in question.
It will not get the student's actual results, but rather return command lines 
that can fetch the results.

\begin{example}\label{email1}
An email from a student\footnote{%
  The student's identity is anonymized and the fictitious name has no 
  relation to any of the student's attributes (other than being a 
  student).%
} may look like the following.
The lines that are marked contain useful data.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,11-12,14}]{text}{email1.txt}
This email should then yield something like the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
ladok student -c DD1317 -r \
  $(canvaslms users -sc DD1317 "Studenten Studentensson" -l | cut -f 3)
canvaslms submissions -c DD1317 \
  -u "Studenten Studentensson"
kthutils forms export restlabb23/24 \
  | grep "Studenten Studentensson"
\end{minted}
\end{example}
The [[ladok]] command fetches the student's results from LADOK.
However, to do that, we need the student's identifier from Canvas.
We can use [[canvaslms]] to fetch student data from Canvas.
Restlabb takes presentations for the intro programming courses after the course 
has ended.
Those TAs can't access the course's Canvas room, so they file their reports in 
a form that can be exported as a spreadsheet.
The [[kthutils forms export]] command can export those results for us.

\begin{example}\label{email2}
Another email:
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,12}]{text}{email2.txt}
This email should then yield the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
ladok student -c XXX -r \
  $(canvaslms users -sc ".*" "^studenten@kth.se" -l | cut -f 3)
canvaslms submissions -c ".*" \
  -u "^studenten@kth.se" \
  -a "granskning"
kthutils forms export restlabb23/24 \
  | grep "Studenten Studentensdotter"
\end{minted}
\end{example}

Why do we want it to output those commands and not the results directly?
We use [[mutt]] for reading and replying to emails.
The [[mutt]] email client uses the user's favourite editor to write the 
reply---Vim.
The idea is then that when we're writing the reply in Vim, we do
\begin{quote}
  [[.!edumail.sh results %]]
\end{quote}
on a blank line to replace the blank line with the output (the commands).
(The [[%]] is a Vim placeholder for the current file, which is the email.)
Then we can select the relevant lines and execute them in the shell:
\begin{quote}
  [['<,'>!bash]]
\end{quote}
If needed, we can edit the lines first---this is why we want the command lines 
and not the results directly.

So, we should be able to pass a filename as argument (so that we can pass 
[[%]]).
That file should contain the email as shown in the examples above.
And it should generate the output as shown in the examples above to stdout.

We'll add a subcommand for each of the lines in the output above.
One that focuses on the [[ladok]] command, one on [[canvaslms]] and the last on 
restlabb.
Then we'll let the [[results]] subcommand call all of these subcommands.

Tools like [[grep]] and [[sed]] are very useful because they either take a file 
as input or read from stdin.
So while we in most cases can read the email from a file, we also want to be 
able to pipe the email to the script.

The subcommand functions will usually be quite straight forward in considering 
this, but for the [[results]] subcommand we need each subcommand called to get 
the same input.
So we must ensure that each of the commands can read stdin, which we do with 
the function~[[file_or_stdin]] (we'll get back to it later).
<<functions>>=
results() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  shift
  ladok "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  missing "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  if wants_restlabb "$file"; then
    restlabb "$file" "$@"
    echo "echo"
  fi
  canvas "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  report "$file" "$@"
}
@

Not every course has a restlabb, though.
Restlabb belongs to the introductory programming courses, [[prgm]] and
[[prgi]]; for the other courses the restlabb lines are only noise to skip
past, and fetching the forms is a slow round trip to KTH's servers
(\cref{RestlabbSection}).
So [[results]] asks for them only when the email concerns one of those two
courses---or when we can't tell the course at all, since then it may well
be one of them.
The test sits in [[results]] rather than in [[restlabb]], so that calling
[[restlabb]] directly still gives the commands for any email.
The course candidates are those of [[course_candidates]]
(\cref{CourseCandidates}): one nick name per line, none if the email gives
no hint of a course.
<<functions>>=
wants_restlabb() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local nicks=$(course_candidates "$file")
  [[ -z "${nicks}" ]] || grep -qxE "prg[im]" <<< "${nicks}"
}
@

The function [[ladok]] will hide the [[ladok]] command from us, so that we
can't use it.
However, this won't be an issue since we don't need to invoke it in this 
script.
We simply write [[ladok]] in the output.
And the execution will happen later in the editor.

\subsection{Overall algorithm}

All the subcommands will have to parse the email.
It's just that they try to get different data out of it.

The most efficient approach would be to parse the email only once.
During that round we extract all the data we need.
Then each of the functions can use that data to construct the output.

However, considering that we might call the subcommands directly, each 
subcommand must ensure the email has been parsed.
This speaks for a more independent design of each and, unfortunately, results 
in the email being parsed multiple times.
This has other advantages, such that in a future design of [[results]], we might 
check which of the subcommands are actually needed.

We'll write functions that extract different data from the email.
These can later be used in the subcommands to construct the output.

\subsection{Function design}

The functions will be designed to take a file as argument.
Or if the email is piped to the script, they will read from stdin.
To achieve this we can rely on the same idea as in the UNIX commands:
if they don't get any file as argument, they read from stdin.
(We can use [[/dev/stdin]] to read from stdin, but keep the structure of the 
code.)
Usually this will work, since we only parse the email once in each function.

However, sometimes we need to read stdin twice.
Then we need to keep a copy of the email in a temporary file.
We can do this by checking if the file argument is an empty string.
If it is, we create a temporary file and read from stdin into that file.
Then we pass the temporary file to the function.
<<functions>>=
file_or_stdin() {
  local file="$1"
  if [[ "$file" = "" ]]; then
    file=$(mktemp)
    cat > "$file"
  fi
  echo "$file"
}
@


\section{Extracting the student's identifier}

We'll start by extracting the student's name or institutional email address.
We prefer the institutional email address, but if that's not available we'll 
use the name.
The function will take the email file as an argument---however, if that is en 
empty string, we want to use stdin.
Since we want to use [[student_name]] if [[student_id]] fails, we need to 
ensure that the email file is passed or piped to both functions.
<<functions>>=
student() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  student_id "$file" \
  || student_name "$file" \
  || any_email "$file"
}
@

To extract the email address, we want to extract the line starting with 
\enquote{To: }.
Then we want to extract the email address from that line.
This is because we're doing this when we reply to the email, thus the student's 
name and email are in the \enquote{To: } field.
We'll try to match a KTH email address.
<<functions>>=
student_id() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local email
  email=$(grep "^To: " "$file" \
          | sed -En "s/^To: [^<]**<?(${USER_FORMAT}@${DOMAIN})>?.*$/\1/p")
  if [[ -n "$email" ]]; then
    echo "$email"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
<<variables>>=
DOMAIN="([a-zA-Z0-9]+\.)?kth\.se"
USER_FORMAT="[a-zA-Z0-9]+"
@

If we didn't find an email address, we'll try to extract the student's name.
If the student sent the email through Canvas, we will not have their KTH email 
and we will have the text \enquote{via Canvas Notifications} appended to the 
name---we want to remove that.
<<functions>>=
student_name() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local name
  name=$(grep "^To: " $file | sed 's/^To: //' \
         | sed -E "s/( via Canvas( Notifications)?)? <.*//")
  if [[ -n "$name" ]]; then
    echo "$name"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
@

If we didn't find any useful data in the email headers,
we can also look for the email address anywhere in the email (body).
This is useful when we pipe text to the script, since then we don't necessarily 
include the headers.
<<functions>>=
any_email() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local email
  email=$(grep -Po "${USER_FORMAT}@${DOMAIN}" "$file" | head -n 1)
  if [[ -n "$email" ]]; then
    echo "$email"
    return 0
  else
    return 1
  fi
}
@

Let's test this on the emails from the examples above.
Running [[student]] on \cref{email1} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "student", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
Running [[student_id]] on \cref{email2} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "student", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}


\section{Extracting the course}

We'll now extract the course from the email.
It's probably too difficult to try to extract the course title.
But we can for sure extract the course code, since that is on a very specific 
format (\eg DD1317).
We can also extract the course's short name, in the case of DD1317 during 2023 
it would be prgi23.

\subsection{Cleaning irrelevant headers}

There are some lines that we don't want to match anything on.
For example, we don't want to match the email identifier in the [[In-Reply-To]] 
header and not the year in the [[Date]] header.
For this, we construct a filter which removes these lines.
(One line of the body goes the same way, for a reason of its own that
we give in \cref{StudentDisclaimer}.)
<<functions>>=
clean() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  grep -vE "^(${IGNORE_HEADERS})" "$file" \
  | <<drop armoured blocks>> \
  | <<drop lines of attachments>> \
  | <<drop the line the mail system wrote>>
}
<<variables>>=
IGNORE_HEADERS="In-Reply-To:|References:|Message-ID:|Content-Type:"
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|^MIME-Version:|^X-Mailer:"
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|^User-Agent:|Date:"
@

We also don't want to match the reply line.
<<variables>>=
IGNORE_HEADERS="${IGNORE_HEADERS}|On .* wrote:$"
@

Some lines are not text at all.
We sign our mail, and a signature is a block of base64 that
[[mutt]] passes on with the rest of the message.
\begin{example}\label{email17}
We answer a colleague about the output of the cron job that reports
resubmitted labs, and sign the answer.
\inputminted[highlightlines={4,5,19-20,25-40}]{text}{email17.txt}
The only things the email names are the assignment group, LAB3, and
the course round in the job's output, ht25.
\end{example}
Base64 is random letters and digits, so every pattern below finds
something in it: LAB9, ABC12 and RZBD7 look like assignment groups to
a pattern that reads capitals followed by digits, XHK1234 like a course
code, and Kprgi24 and ab12 like course nick names.
Before we dropped the block, \cref{email17} gave ten time-tracking
labels (\cref{LabelsSection}), nine of them from the signature.

The armour lines tell where the block begins, and its contents tell
where it ends: base64, blank lines and the few header lines that
[[gpg]] may write after the first armour line.
So we drop the block from its first armour line up to the first line
that is none of these, and we drop the last armour line if that is
what ends it.
A block that is cut short---by someone trimming a quote---then ends
where the cut is, and the text after it is still read.
The prefix of quote characters covers signatures in the messages we
answer, since we quote them too.
<<drop armoured blocks>>=
awk -v begin="^${QUOTE_PREFIX}${PGP_ARMOUR_BEGIN}" \
    -v end="^${QUOTE_PREFIX}${PGP_ARMOUR_END}" \
    -v inside="^${QUOTE_PREFIX}(${BASE64_LINE}|${PGP_ARMOUR_HEADER})?$" '
  $0 ~ begin { armour = 1; next }
  armour && $0 ~ end { armour = 0; next }
  armour && $0 ~ inside { next }
  { armour = 0; print }'
<<variables>>=
QUOTE_PREFIX=$'[> \t]*'
PGP_ARMOUR_BEGIN="-----BEGIN PGP (SIGNATURE|MESSAGE|PUBLIC KEY BLOCK)-----"
PGP_ARMOUR_END="-----END PGP [A-Z ]+-----"
PGP_ARMOUR_HEADER="(Version|Comment|Hash|Charset): .*"
BASE64_LINE="[A-Za-z0-9+/=]+"
@ A signed message in the old inline style starts with
[[-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----]], and that is not among the
blocks we drop: what follows that line is the text, and only the
signature at its end is base64.

Attachments are base64 too, without armour.
What marks their lines is their shape: base64 is cut into lines of a
fixed length, 76 characters in MIME and 64 in a signature, with no
space in them.
A line of text that long without a space does not occur in the
languages we write in, so we also drop every line of 60 such
characters or more, wherever it is.
<<drop lines of attachments>>=
grep -vE "^${QUOTE_PREFIX}${BASE64_LINE_OF_ATTACHMENT}$"
<<variables>>=
BASE64_LINE_OF_ATTACHMENT="[A-Za-z0-9+/]{60,}={0,2}"
@ The last line of an attachment is usually shorter and stays.
That leaves a few characters of random text, and the patterns
below must cope with it anyway, so they too are narrow
(\cref{AssignmentGroups,CourseCandidates}).

\subsection{Telling a student's email from a colleague's}
\label{StudentDisclaimer}

One line in an email is worth reading for who wrote it rather than for
what it says.
KTH appends a disclaimer to mail from outside---\enquote{avsändaren
inte är anställd vid Kungliga Tekniska högskolan}, in Swedish and
English---and it is the mail system, not the sender, that puts it
there.
<<functions>>=
is_student() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  grep -qiE "${STUDENT_DISCLAIMER}" "$file"
}
<<variables>>=
STUDENT_DISCLAIMER="inte är anställd vid Kungliga Tekniska"
STUDENT_DISCLAIMER="${STUDENT_DISCLAIMER}|is not employed by KTH"
@

The test reads the raw email rather than the cleaned one, and it does
not care whether the line is quoted:
in a reply to a student the disclaimer sits inside the quoted message,
which is exactly the case we want to recognise.

What the line proves runs one way only.
It is there because the sender is not an employee, so seeing it settles
that a student wrote the email; \emph{not} seeing it settles nothing,
since it is a recent addition and the mail may be old or forwarded.
\Cref{email2,email3} are student emails without it.
Everything below therefore uses [[is_student]] to license a guess, never
to forbid one.

Reading the line for who wrote the email is one half of treating it
right; the other is not to read it for what it says.
Its words are the mail system's, and one of them is
\enquote{anställd}---an employment word, and the assistant test
(\cref{LabelsSection}) looks for those.
With the line left in, every student has written about employment, and
the test's other half decides alone:
a student who mentions the assistant who graded their lab has then
sent us assistant mail.
So [[clean]] drops the line, quoted or not, and every keyword test
reads the email without it.
<<drop the line the mail system wrote>>=
grep -viE "${STUDENT_DISCLAIMER}"
@ [[is_student]] is unaffected, since it reads the raw email.
Only the line with the phrase goes.
The rest of the disclaimer---who answers for the opinions in the
message---matches nothing we look for, and a pattern wide enough to
cover it would sooner or later cover something a student wrote.

The line says something sharper than \enquote{a student}, which is
worth recording even though nothing uses it yet:
the sender is not employed \emph{by KTH}.
A student who already assists on a course is an employee and loses the
disclaimer, so on mail about assistants the line separates an applicant
from someone who holds a position---\cref{email5}, an application, has
it, and the contract thread (\cref{email6,email7,email8}) does not.

\subsection{Extracting course identifiers from clean email}

This leaves us with the following construction.
We first match for course codes, then for course nick names.
Finally, we also try to guess the course based on keywords in the email.
Let's look at the example emails again.
\Cref{email1} should yield something like
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} should yield something like
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

These two emails show the two ways we can learn the course:
\cref{email1} names it by code, \cref{email2} only describes it.
Two more emails show why we must keep the two apart.
\begin{example}\label{email4}
A student asking about a missing grade names the course in the body rather 
than in the subject; the subject names the module.
\inputminted[highlightlines={3,4,13,14}]{text}{email4.txt}
The student has told us which course they mean, so we want exactly that 
course, DD1317, and not every course that the words in the email would 
suggest.
\end{example}
(The header lines of \cref{email4} are what our [[mutt]] writes today.
The [[Date:]] header and the \enquote{On \ldots\ wrote:} line are among 
the lines that [[clean]] removes, so they don't disturb the matching.)
\begin{example}\label{email5}
A student applying to become a teaching assistant names five course codes: 
the one they apply for and four they have experience from.
\inputminted[highlightlines={3,4,14,17,19-20}]{text}{email5.txt}
Only DD1301 is a course we teach.
The other codes can't be what we want to look up results for---they are 
not our courses---so we want DD1301 alone.
\end{example}

Since any matches may occur several times in the email, we'll sort them and 
remove duplicates.
And to deal with output containing several lines, we'll make a disjunct regex 
with [[make_disjunct_regex]] discussed below.
<<functions>>=
course() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  (course_code "$file"; echo; course_nick "$file") \
  | sort -u \
  | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ This function output what we just saw above.
The [[course]] function calls [[course_code]] and [[course_nick]] to extract 
the course code and course nick name, respectively.
Their outputs are just concatenated.

Both functions rely on the same knowledge of which keywords indicate which 
course; they differ only in what they print for it, a code or a nick name.
So we keep that knowledge in one place, [[course_families]], and let both 
derive their output from it.
It prints one \emph{family} per line:
the courses that share keywords, and that we therefore cannot tell apart from 
the text of an email---[[prgm]] (DD1310) and [[prgi]] (DD1317) being the 
typical pair.
Members are named by nick name, the shorter and more stable of the two names.
<<functions>>=
course_families() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  <<narrow [[file]] to what we may guess from>>
  if <<test for datintro terms>>; then
    echo "datintro progd"
  fi
  if <<test for programming terms>>; then
    echo "prgm prgi"
  fi
  if <<test for crypto terms>>; then
    echo "tilkry"
  fi
  if <<test for science terms>>; then
    echo "vetcyb"
  fi
}
@ Note that we don't use [[elif]]: a student might write about several courses 
in one email.

\begin{example}\label{email11}
An administrator writes about the credit card that pays for our
subscriptions.
\inputminted[highlightlines={4,21,44,65,83}]{text}{email11.txt}
No course is named and none is meant.
\end{example}

These keywords are ordinary words, and that is the point:
they are what a student writes who has not named the course.
It is also their danger, because an email is more than what its author
wrote.
Under a colleague's signature stands the name of our school, which ends
in \enquote{Computer Science}, and that is the science course's keyword;
below it the quoted thread and the ordinary English of administration.
\Cref{email11} collects three such hits and comes out belonging to four
courses, having nothing to do with any of them.

So we let the sender decide how far the guess may reach.
When the disclaimer tells us a student wrote the email
(\cref{StudentDisclaimer}), we search all of it, as before.
Otherwise we search the subject line alone:
a student who describes a course does it in the subject too---and the
three false hits in \cref{email11} are all in the body.
This is a restriction on guessing only.
Course codes and nick names are read from the whole email whoever wrote
it, since naming a course is deliberate.
<<narrow [[file]] to what we may guess from>>=
if ! is_student "$file"; then
  local subject=$(mktemp)
  grep -i "^Subject: " "$file" > "$subject"
  file="${subject}"
fi
@ Rewriting [[file]] rather than the tests keeps the keywords in one
place:
[[clean]] leaves a subject line alone, so the four tests below read the
narrowed email without knowing that it was narrowed.
It also has to be here and not in [[<<look for>>]], which
[[assignment_groups]] and the assistant test share:
\cref{email10} states its appointment in the body, and narrowing that
test to the subject would lose it.

The course code of each nick name is a table.
<<variables>>=
declare -A COURSE_CODES=(
  [datintro]=DD1301 [progd]=DD1337
  [prgm]=DD1310     [prgi]=DD1317
  [tilkry]=DD2520
  [vetcyb]=DA2215
)
@

We'll also need the table read backwards: the nick of a course code.
A code the table doesn't know is a name in its own right and passes 
through unchanged---a student naming DD1320 has named a course, just not 
one of ours.
We upper-case the code first, since students tend to write codes in lower 
case.
<<functions>>=
nick_of_course_code() {
  local code="${1^^}" nick
  for nick in "${!COURSE_CODES[@]}"; do
    if [[ "${COURSE_CODES[$nick]}" == "${code}" ]]; then
      echo "${nick}"
      return
    fi
  done
  echo "${code}"
}
@

\subsubsection{Named courses beat guessed ones}\label{CourseCandidates}

\Cref{email4,email5} tell us that a course the student \emph{names} 
outranks anything we \emph{guess}.
The keyword tests are a fallback for the common case that the student names 
nothing (\cref{email2}); once a code or a nick name is in the email, adding 
the keyword families only adds noise: \cref{email4} would get [[prgm]] on top 
of the [[prgi]] the student wrote.
So we first collect the named courses.
Codes are translated to nick names, so that a course named both ways 
counts once; a nick name loses its year, since the year says which 
\emph{round} was named, not which course.
<<functions>>=
named_courses() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local named=$( (<<courses named in [[file]]>>) | sort -u )
  <<keep only the known courses among [[named]], if there are any>>
}
<<courses named in [[file]]>>=
clean "$file" | grep -Po "${COURSE_CODE}" \
| while read -r code; do nick_of_course_code "${code}"; done
clean "$file" | grep -Po "${COURSE_NICK}" | sed -E 's/[0-9]{2}$//'
@

A nick name may also come without its year.
\begin{example}\label{email12}
A student of datintro sends us their solution to an optional exercise.
\inputminted[highlightlines={4,11,15,17,31}]{text}{email12.txt}
The subject names the course, but without a year.
\end{example}
[[COURSE_NICK]] reads a nick name with its year, as in [[prgi23]], so
\cref{email12} named nothing, and we guessed over the whole email,
since a student wrote it:
\enquote{bash} and \enquote{script} gave the datintro family,
\enquote{specifies} the programming one, and the email counted for four
courses.
For our own courses we don't need the year, since we know their nick
names: we look for them as whole words.
The word boundaries keep the nick names with a year out, as a digit is a
word character; [[COURSE_NICK]] already finds those.
A subject starts with a capital, as in \cref{email12}, so we match
regardless of case and print the nick name as the table has it.
<<courses named in [[file]]>>=
clean "$file" | grep -Pio "\b(${KNOWN_NICKS})\b" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
<<variables>>=
KNOWN_NICKS=$(IFS='|'; echo "${!COURSE_CODES[*]}")
@

\Cref{email5} adds a second rule: among the named courses, the ones we 
teach outrank the ones we don't.
A student who names one of our courses together with others has told us 
which one concerns us; the others are context (previous experience, in 
\cref{email5}).
Only when none of the named courses is ours do we let the unknown ones 
through---then they are the best we have, and the user's rules file may 
still know what to do with them (see \cref{LabelsSection}).
The guard on [[named]] matters:
[[printf]] with a format but no arguments still prints the format once, 
which would turn \enquote{no course} into a blank line.
<<keep only the known courses among [[named]], if there are any>>=
local nick known=""
for nick in ${named}; do
  [[ -n "${COURSE_CODES[$nick]}" ]] && known+="${nick}"$'\n'
done
[[ -n "${named}" ]] && printf '%s\n' ${known:-${named}}
return 0
@

The candidates for the course are then the named courses, or, only if 
there are none, the members of the families that the keywords suggest.
When only keywords identify the course we cannot tell [[prgm]] from 
[[prgi]] and we output both---old students of [[prgm]] do still write.
The unquoted [[${named}]] and the [[tr]] both serve the same output 
format: one nick name per line.
<<functions>>=
course_candidates() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local named=$(named_courses "$file")
  if [[ -n "${named}" ]]; then
    echo "${named}"
  else
    course_families "$file" | tr ' ' '\n'
  fi
}
@

\subsubsection{From candidates to codes and nick names}

The [[course_code]] function prints the code of each candidate.
A course we know is looked up in the table; an unknown \emph{code} is its 
own code and passes through; an unknown \emph{nick name} would have no
code we could print, so the [[grep]] against [[COURSE_CODE]] drops it.
(We only read the nick names we know, see below, so none should get
this far; the [[grep]] is what makes sure.)
Since many might match, we sort the output and remove duplicates.
Finally, we make a disjunct regex from the output.
<<functions>>=
course_code() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local nick
  for nick in $(course_candidates "$file"); do
    echo "${COURSE_CODES[$nick]:-${nick}}"
  done | grep -Px "${COURSE_CODE}" \
    | sort -u \
    | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ If we run this function on the examples we get the following.
\Cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email4} names the course, so the family is not guessed:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email4.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email5} names five courses, of which we keep the one that is ours:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_code", "email5.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

The [[course_nick]] function does the same for nick names.
Each candidate contributes the rounds the student wrote (a [[prgi23]] in 
the email) and, for a course we know, also the recent rounds.
The latter is a guess at the course round the student means; usually they 
omit the year, see below.
<<functions>>=
course_nick() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local nick
  for nick in $(course_candidates "$file"); do
    [[ -n "${COURSE_CODES[$nick]}" ]] && echo "${nick}${YEARS_REGEX}"
    clean "$file" \
    | grep -Po "${NOT_IN_WORD_BEFORE}${nick}[0-9]{2}${NOT_IN_WORD_AFTER}"
  done | sort -u \
    | make_disjunct_regex
}
@ Running the [[course_nick]] function on the examples yields the following.
\Cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email4} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email4.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email5} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course_nick", "email5.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

For the patterns, the important part is to look around the match, so
that we don't match substrings of other data.
For instance, students usually write the course code with lower case and that 
can be mistaken for a course nick name.
So we want a lookahead to ensure we get space or punctuation after the course 
code, and a lookbehind to ensure the same before it:
in \cref{email17}'s signature, [[xHk1234+]] ends like a course code,
and only the letter in front tells us that it doesn't begin like one.
<<variables>>=
NOT_IN_WORD_BEFORE="(?<![A-Za-z0-9])"
NOT_IN_WORD_AFTER="(?![A-Za-z0-9])"
COURSE_CODE="${NOT_IN_WORD_BEFORE}[A-Za-z]{2,3}[0-9]{3,4}[A-Za-z]?"
COURSE_CODE="${COURSE_CODE}${NOT_IN_WORD_AFTER}"
@ Note that we need the [[-P]] option for these lookarounds in the regex
to work with [[grep]].

A course code is recognizable whoever's course it is, and we keep
recognizing the codes we don't know:
a student who names only a course of someone else's has still named a
course, and we must not guess one of ours instead
(\cref{CourseCandidates}).
A nick name is not recognizable in the same way.
The pattern for it used to be any word followed by two digits, and
that is a lot of words:
the [[ht25]] of a course round in \cref{email17}, the [[stud42]] of a
user name in an email address, and the [[ab12]] in a signature.
Each counted as a named course, with its year stripped; when no
course of ours was named, it became a label ([[ht]], [[stud]],
[[ab]]), and since a named course turns off the guesses, it also hid
the courses that the email's words suggest.
So we only read nick names that we know, with their year.
A nick name of someone else's course, such as [[tilda23]], no longer
counts as named; a rules file that knows the course can still label
the email (\cref{LabelRules}).
<<variables>>=
COURSE_NICK="${NOT_IN_WORD_BEFORE}(${KNOWN_NICKS})[0-9]{2}"
COURSE_NICK="${COURSE_NICK}${NOT_IN_WORD_AFTER}"
@

Usually the students omit the year they took the course, particularly if they 
took it the last one or two year.
(Those from many years ago tend to write when they took it.)
We need the year so that we can match the course nick name with the last few 
years (instead of all throughout time) when the student gives only the course 
but not the year they took it.
<<variables>>=
YEAR=$(date +%y)
YEARS_REGEX="($((YEAR-2))|$((YEAR-1))|$YEAR)"
@

A lot of the time, the students don't include the course code or nick name in 
the email.
So we have to guess what course it is based on the content of the email.
For the programming course, there are a few things to look for.
Firstly, everything is in Swedish.
Secondly, we look for common terms for the course (programming, Python) and 
then the unique terms for assignments (\enquote{datorprov} as a specific name 
rather than \enquote{labbar}, the latter being too generic).
The crypto and science courses are for fourth and fifth year students, so they 
are in English---although Swedish students sometimes write their emails in 
Swedish if they have figured out that I'm fluent in Swedish.
<<test for programming terms>>=
   <<look for>> "programm?ering|python|DD1310|DD1317" \
|| <<look for>> "datorprov|projekt|p-uppgift|gransk|${PROJECT_SPEC}" \
|| <<look for>> "lab(b|oration) [1-6]|Excel" \
|| <<look for>> "restlab"
<<test for datintro terms>>=
   <<look for>> "data?intro|DD13(01|37)" \
|| <<look for>> "ssh|terminal|student-shell|bash|shell|Public" \
|| <<look for>> "rättning|\bs[ck]ript" \
|| <<look for>> "git|latex"
<<test for crypto terms>>=
   <<look for>> "[kc]rypto|DD2520" \
|| <<look for>> "ciphertext|plaintext|breaking|[ck]rypo?tanalys" \
|| <<look for>> "AES|ProVerif|usability" \
|| <<look for>> "INL1 ?(Written|Oral)"
<<test for science terms>>=
<<look for>> "DA2215|TAMOS|scien|project|seminar|formal|report"
@

The \enquote{spec} of the programming course is the specification of the
project, which students call \enquote{specen} or write out as
\enquote{specifikation}.
The bare stem also begins ordinary English words: the student of
\cref{email12} \enquote{specifies} two options and a \enquote{specified}
number of seconds.
So we let the stem end only in those two ways.
The ending is in a lookahead, so that a match is still just
\enquote{spec}; that matters when we extract it as the name of an
assignment ([[ASSIGNMENT]], \cref{AssignmentsSection}).
<<variables>>=
PROJECT_SPEC="\bspec(?=(en|ifi[ck]ation)?\b)"
@

We want the [[grep]] to be quiet and case insensitive.
We also want to use the Perl style regex syntax ([[-P]]), as mentioned above.
We'll need to clean the email from irrelevant headers before we look for the 
keywords.
<<look for>>=
clean "$file" | grep -qiP
@

\subsection{Creating a disjunct regex}

To make a disjunct regex, we simply concatenate the lines with a pipe (a sign 
for \enquote{or} in regex).
\begin{example}\label{disjunct-regex-example}
We want to turn
\begin{minted}{text}
DD1317
prgi23
\end{minted}
into
\begin{minted}{text}
(DD1317|prgi23)
\end{minted}
\end{example}
There are two special cases to consider:
We must make sure to not end with a pipe.
And if there is a blank line, we'll get two pipes in a row, which we also want 
to avoid.
So we simply remove these two cases when they occur.
<<functions>>=
make_disjunct_regex() {
  echo -n "("
  tr '\n' '|' | sed 's/||/|/g' | sed 's/|$//'
  echo -n ")"
}
@ Let's test this on \cref{disjunct-regex-example} above.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(
  "(echo DD1317; echo prgi23) | ./edumail make_disjunct_regex",
  shell=True, capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

If we run this on the emails from the examples above, we should get the 
following output.
Running [[course]] on \cref{email1} yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
This is correct.
The student gave the course code, but not which course round.
The email also contains keywords for the programming courses, but since the 
course is named, the family is not guessed and [[prgm]] stays out.
(The years in the regex are calculated from the current year, when we compile 
this document, but the email examples are older than that---from when I wrote 
the first version of this script.
But as long as I read the student's email within a year or two, the regex will 
likely match the course round.)

Running [[course]] on \cref{email2} should yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "course", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
This is correct, since the email doesn't contain any course code.
In fact, \cref{email2} doesn't identify any course at all, so the keyword 
families are all we have.


\section{Extracting assignments}

We also want to extract the assignments.
There are two alternatives: individual assignments or assignment groups (LADOK 
modules).

\subsection{Assignment groups}\label{AssignmentGroups}

The assignment groups are the easiest to extract, they're always named
something like LAB1, LAB2, LAB3, \etc.
We can match those with a regex.
It used to be any three capitals or more followed by digits, which is
also what \cref{email17}'s signature is full of.
Dropping the signature removed that source, but not the pattern's
appetite: in an email as it is stored, the headers in which mail
servers note how they passed it on give [[TLS1]] and [[SHA384]] the
same way.
So we list the kinds of module we track---labs, written
assignments, seminars and the Excel and mathematics modules of the
programming course, plus exams and projects for the courses that
have them---and accept only those.
A course with another kind of module needs its prefix added here.
The pattern must also stand alone, as a course code must, so that LAB9
inside a longer string of letters and digits isn't taken for a module
either.
The price is that a module written together with more letters, such
as [[LAB3a]], is not read either; punctuation after it, as in
[[LAB3:]] or [[LAB3.]], is fine.
<<variables>>=
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_PREFIXES="LAB|INL|SEM|KAL|MAT|TEN|PRO"
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP="(${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_PREFIXES})[0-9]+"
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP="${NOT_IN_WORD_BEFORE}${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP}${NOT_IN_WORD_AFTER}"
@

Now we'll do like we've done before and grep for these in the email, getting 
only the matching parts.
<<functions>>=
assignment_groups() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local groups=$( (<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>) \
                  | sort -u )
  if [[ -n "$groups" ]]; then
    echo "$groups"
  else
    echo "^${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_DEFAULT}"
  fi | make_disjunct_regex
}
<<variables>>=
ASSIGNMENT_GROUP_DEFAULT="(LAB|INL|SEM)[0-9]+"
@

The first thing we'll do is to look for things that look like assignment groups 
in the email.
We note that we must not match a course code, should one ever start
with one of the prefixes in [[ASSIGNMENT_GROUP]], so we remove anything
that also matches a course code.
The keyword tests below are course specific, so we also determine the course 
here---once, since [[course]] is the expensive part of this script and the 
tests below would otherwise each run it again.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
local courses=$(course "$file")
<<literal assignment groups in [[file]]>>
@ The literal search is a chunk of its own because it is also all that
mail from a program gets (\cref{LabelsSection}): what such mail names it
means, but its words are not to be guessed from.
<<literal assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
clean "$file" \
| grep -Po "${ASSIGNMENT_GROUP}" \
| grep -Pv "${COURSE_CODE}"
@

This will rarely be sufficient, so we also look for keywords that indicate 
which assignment group it is.
This is easy for the datintro and vetcyb courses, they only have one assignment 
group, so we can reuse the tests from above.
However, a keyword in one course might indicate a different assignment group in 
another course, so we'd better make these course specific.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
if grep -qP "(DD13(01|37)|datintro)" <<< "${courses}"; then
  if <<test for datintro terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
fi
if grep -qP "(DA2215|vetcyb)" <<< "${courses}"; then
  if <<test for science terms>>; then
    echo "INL1"
  fi
fi
@ Note that we don't use [[elif]]: a student might email about several courses 
in one email.

For the other courses we must be more specific, since they have different 
modules corresponding to different assignments.
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>=
if grep -qP "(DD131[07]|prg[im])" <<< "${courses}"; then
  if <<test for programming LAB1 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
  if <<test for prgoramming LAB2 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB2"
  fi
  if <<test for programming LAB3 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB3"
  fi
  if <<test for programming KAL1 terms>>; then
    echo "KAL1"
  fi
fi
if grep -qP "(DD2520|tilkry)" <<< "${courses}"; then
  if <<test for tilkry LAB1 terms>>; then
    echo "LAB1"
  fi
  if <<test for tilkry INL1 terms>>; then
    echo "INL1"
  fi
fi
@

LAB1 for the programming course is a series of labs.
<<test for programming LAB1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "lab(b|oration) [1-6]"
@ LAB2 is the so called Datorprov, an exam.
<<test for prgoramming LAB2 terms>>=
<<look for>> "datorprov|(kontroll|kort)skrivning|prov|tenta"
@ LAB3 is the project assignment.
<<test for programming LAB3 terms>>=
<<look for>> "projekt|p-uppgift|gransk|${PROJECT_SPEC}"
@ KAL1 is the Excel part, consists of four labs in Excel.
<<test for programming KAL1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "Excel"
@

Let's look at the security courses.
LAB1 for applied crypto (tilkry) is a series of mandatory and optional labs.
But it also contains the usabilty and design considerations seminars.
<<test for tilkry LAB1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "AES|ProVerif|usabilty|design|consideration|VSD|seminar"
@ INL1 on that course consists of the creatively named assignments
\begin{itemize}
  \item INL1 Written,
  \item INL1 Oral, and
  \item INL1 Quiz.
\end{itemize}
<<test for tilkry INL1 terms>>=
<<look for>> "INL1 ?(Written|Oral|Quiz)|quiz|written|oral"
@

None of the examples (\cref{email1,email2}) contain any assignment groups.
But they do contain individual assignments.
\begin{example}[Assignment groups]\label{email3}
We can consider the following email that contains assignment groups.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,13}]{text}{email3.txt}
This email should then yield the following output:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms submissions \
  -c ".*" \
  -u "^studuser@kth.se" \
  -A LAB2
\end{minted}
\end{example}

Let's test [[assignment_groups]] on \cref{email3} above.
It yields the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignment_groups", "email3.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

Let's also try it on \cref{email1} above.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignment_groups", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
So we see that it's important to have a good default.
And \cref{email2}:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignment_groups", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

\subsection{Assignments}\label{AssignmentsSection}

For individual assignments we need a list of names to look for.
We can look at \cref{email3} again, since it has both an assignment and an 
assignment group.
But we will also revisit \cref{email1,email2}, since both have assignments (but 
no assignment groups).

The approach will be similar to that of [[assignment_groups]], but we'll have 
to grep for known assignment names.
However, if we don't find any, we won't output a default regex.
<<functions>>=
assignments() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local assns=$(clean "$file" \
                | grep -iPo "${ASSIGNMENT}" \
                | sort -u)
  if [[ -n "$assns" ]]; then
    echo "$assns"
  fi | make_disjunct_regex
}
@

The known assignments names are as follows.
<<variables>>=
ASSIGNMENT="granskn|lab(b|oration) ?[0-9]+|${PROJECT_SPEC}|p-uppgift|projekt"
ASSIGNMENT="${ASSIGNMENT}|datorprov"
@

Let's test this on the emails from the examples above.
Running [[assignments]] on \cref{email1} yield the following output:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignments", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The output from \cref{email2} is the following:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignments", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The output from \cref{email3} is the following:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "assignments", "email3.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}


\section{Constructing the command lines}

Now that we have some building blocks, we can construct the command lines.
Since we can't get perfect results from the email, we'll use variables for the 
different identifiers.
That way we can easily correct them in one place, instead of several.
\begin{example}[Actually desired output]
In fact, we want the output of \cref{email1} to be the following.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "results", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout.decode().strip())
\end{pycode}
This way, if there is any error in the identifiers, we can easily correct them 
in one place.
\end{example}

Now, the functions should be possible to use individually, but we don't want 
the same variables several times.
This gives us the following construction.
<<functions>>=
ladok() {
  <<output variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[ladok]] command from email>>
}
canvas() {
  <<output variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[canvaslms]] command from email>>
}
restlabb() {
  <<output variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[restlabb]] command from email>>
}
missing() {
  <<output variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[missing]] command from email>>
}
report() {
  <<output variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[report]] command from email>>
}
@


\subsection{Outputting the variables}

We only want to output the variables if they haven't already been output.
To achieve this we'll use a flag variable.
<<output variables if not already done>>=
local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
if [[ -z "$VARS_ALREADY_OUTPUT" ]]; then
  VARS_ALREADY_OUTPUT=1
  <<echo the needed variables>>
fi
@

\subsection{Generate the [[ladok]] command}

The [[ladok]] command is quite straight forward.
However, we need the course code for LADOK, not any identifier.
Then we can get the student identifier from Canvas.
Note that if the student is in several courses and the regex [[COURSE]] match, 
then we need to filter it down to one ([[uniq]]).
Also, if the regex matches several students, we need to loop over them all.
<<construct [[ladok]] command from email>>=
echo 'for student_id in $(canvaslms users -sc "${COURSE}" "${STUDENT}" -l \'
echo '                    | cut -f 3 | uniq)'
echo 'do'
echo '  ladok student -c "${COURSE_CODE}" -r "${student_id}"'
echo '  echo'
echo 'done'
<<echo the needed variables>>=
echo "COURSE=\"$(course_nick $file)\""
echo "COURSE_CODE=\"$(course_code $file)\""
echo "STUDENT=\"$(student $file)\""
@

\subsection{Generate the [[canvaslms]] command}\label{CanvasCommand}

For the [[canvaslms]] command we can do some output modification to make it 
useful.
<<construct [[canvaslms]] command from email>>=
echo 'canvaslms submissions \'
echo '  -c "${COURSE}" \'
echo '  -u "${STUDENT}" \'
<<specify assignment or assignment groups if they exist>>
<<cut the output to only include the relevant columns>>
@

We usually don't need all columns in its output.
We want the first, to see which year the submission belongs to.
Then the second column, the assignment itself.
We don't need the third column, which is the student name or email.
Finally, we want all columns from the fourth and onwards: grades and dates.
<<cut the output to only include the relevant columns>>=
echo '  | cut -f 1-2,4-'
@

We can't specify assignment and assignment group at the same time.
They are mutually exclusive.
We can make it choose the one that is not empty.

We could make them print conditionally.
But that turned out to be rather difficult.
A better idea is to make [[-a]] and [[-A]] not mutually exclusive in 
[[canvaslms]].
<<specify assignment or assignment groups if they exist>>=
echo '  -a "${ASSIGN}" \'
echo '  -A "${ASSIGN_GRPS}" \'
<<echo the needed variables>>=
echo "ASSIGN=\"$(assignments $file)\""
echo "ASSIGN_GRPS=\"$(assignment_groups $file)\""
@


\subsection{Generate the restlabb command}\label{RestlabbSection}

We simply grep for the student in the restlabb export.
In fact, we want to make the same rewrites as the normal script does so that we 
can quickly report any missing results if needed.
Sometimes we miss a result because there are two results for the same student 
and same assignment in the same run.
We do this for all forms that we're interested in.

For the rewriting, some forms use different formats and, thus, require 
different rewriters.
Originally there were two groups: one rewriter for restlabb and one for 
labweek.
However, the different years use different form formats too.
So we version the rewriters the same way as we do for the forms:
a rewriter is named exactly like the form it rewrites (\eg [[restlabb26/27]] 
for the form [[restlabb26/27]]).
Consequently, we can't pipe all forms through one rewriter.
We must pair each form with its own rewriter.

This also means that we can't concatenate the exports of all forms into one 
pipeline, as we did before.
The rewriter must see rows of a single format, and its output is a sequence 
of [[echo]] commands, which we can't sort afterwards.
So we output one pipeline per form instead.
That's also more convenient when we want to edit the commands in the editor: 
the form name is right there in the command, rather than hidden in a variable.
<<construct [[restlabb]] command from email>>=
<<fetch forms data if not already done>>
for form in ${RESTLABB_FORMS}; do
  echo "kthutils forms export ${form} \\"
  echo '  | <<resort chronologically>> \'
  echo '  | grep "${STUDENT}" \'
  echo "  | kthutils forms rewriter rewrite $(form_rewriter ${form})"
done
@ Note that we use double quotes on the first and last lines above, that's 
because we want to expand the form and the rewriter to include their values in 
the output.
Whereas the [[STUDENT]] variable should remain a variable in the output.
Also note that [[grep]] for the student has the side effect of dropping the 
header row of the export.

Let's define the variables we need for handling restlabb.
We can simply look through all the forms that we have.
At least those that match \enquote{restlabb} and \enquote{labweek}.
Since [[kthutils forms ls]] lists the forms oldest first, we'll process the
forms in chronological order.

We don't want to fetch this data when the script loads.
Each [[kthutils]] call is a round trip to KTH's servers, taking seconds.
That was acceptable when the script was only used for the results
subcommands, which need the data anyway.
But the [[labels]] subcommand (\cref{LabelsSection}) is called for every
email we open in [[mutt]], and it never needs the forms.
So we fetch the data at first use instead, using the same flag-variable
idiom as [[<<output variables if not already done>>]].
The memoization works across the [[$(form_rewriter ...)]] command
substitutions in [[<<construct [[restlabb]] command from email>>]]:
the fetch runs in the function's own shell, so the subshells inherit both
the flag and the fetched variables.
<<fetch forms data if not already done>>=
if [[ -z "$FORMS_ALREADY_FETCHED" ]]; then
  FORMS_ALREADY_FETCHED=1
  RESTLABB_FORMS=$(kthutils forms ls \
                   | grep -iE "restlabb2[5-9]/2[6-9]|labweek202[6-9]")
  <<fetch the [[REWRITERS]] from the [[kthutils]] config>>
fi
@

Within a form, the newest entry is first.
Since sometimes a student may present and fail before finally passing, it's 
good to have the entries in chronological order.
This way we report the results in that order, consequently the final pass will 
overwrite the previous fail.
We can do this by sorting the output:
The first key is the date and time, in ISO format, so it sorts 
lexicographically.
As we get it from the forms export, it's sorted newest to oldest;
so we just need to sort it.
<<resort chronologically>>=
sort
@

\subsubsection{Matching a form with its rewriter}

The rewriters are stored in the [[kthutils]] config, under 
[[forms.rewriter]].
We don't want to maintain a table mapping forms to rewriters here, since that 
would have to be updated every year.
Instead we derive the rewriter from the naming convention above:
if there is a rewriter named like the form, we use it.
Otherwise we fall back to a rewriter named like the group ([[restlabb]] or 
[[labweek]]), which is how a rewriter shared by several years would be named.
Since the year suffix always starts with a digit, we can strip everything from 
the first digit to get the group name.
This way, adding a new year with its own rewriter to the [[kthutils]] config is 
all that's needed, and a year without a specific rewriter can still be handled 
by a shared one.
<<functions>>=
form_rewriter() {
  local form="$1"
  <<fetch forms data if not already done>>
  if echo "${REWRITERS}" | grep -qxF "${form}"; then
    echo "${form}"
  else
    echo "${form%%[0-9]*}"
  fi
}
@

Note that [[form_rewriter]] also starts with
[[<<fetch forms data if not already done>>]]:
it needs [[REWRITERS]] when called directly as a subcommand, without going
through [[restlabb]] first.

To know which rewriters exist, we list the config keys under
[[forms.rewriter]].
Each rewriter has several settings, so the same name appears several times and
we must remove duplicates.
The rewriter name can contain a slash (\eg [[restlabb26/27]]) but never a dot,
which is the separator in the config path.
<<fetch the [[REWRITERS]] from the [[kthutils]] config>>=
REWRITERS=$(kthutils config forms.rewriter \
            | sed -En "s/^forms\.rewriter\.([^.]+)\..*/\1/p" | sort -u)
@


\subsection{One summary per grading scheme}\label{GradingSchemes}

The missing and report commands below both use [[canvaslms results]],
which summarizes each assignment group (LADOK module) into one grade.
How to summarize is up to a summary module ([[-S]]).
For most modules of our courses, [[canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey]]
does it: every assignment must pass, the grade is the average.

LAB1 of the applied crypto course (tilkry) is different.
It consists of mandatory labs, graded P/F, and optional labs, scored
between 0 and 2.5; the sum of the optional scores sets the grade.
Averaging those gives garbage, so canvaslms has a summary module of its
own for it: [[canvaslms.grades.tilkryLAB1]].
It also knows which LAB1 labs are missing, since only the mandatory ones
can be.

But [[canvaslms results]] takes one summary module per call.
So for tilkry we need two calls: one for LAB1 with the tilkry module, and
one for the other modules with the ordinary.
It's not enough to add the LAB1 call, the ordinary call must leave LAB1
out, or it prints the garbage summary alongside the right one---and it's
the ordinary call's LAB1 row we'd risk reporting to LADOK.

\begin{example}\label{email14}
A student of the applied crypto course writes about both LAB1 (the AES
lab) and INL1 (the quiz).
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,12}]{text}{email14.txt}
This email needs both calls, and the ordinary one without LAB1.
\end{example}

We'll ask several times whether an email concerns tilkry, so we give the
question a name.
The candidates are those of [[course_candidates]] (\cref{CourseCandidates}),
one nick name per line.
<<functions>>=
concerns_course() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  course_candidates "$file" | grep -qxF "$2"
}
@

Tilkry has two modules, LAB1 and INL1.
So we don't have to cut LAB1 out of [[ASSIGN_GRPS]] for the ordinary
call: we can name the modules directly and make one call per module.
That leaves [[ASSIGN_GRPS]] as it is, for the other courses and for the
[[canvaslms submissions]] command, which should list LAB1 too.
<<variables>>=
TILKRY_MODULES="LAB1 INL1"
@

We call each such call a \emph{part}.
[[summary_parts]] decides which parts an email needs:
[[ordinary]], the one call with [[ASSIGN_GRPS]] that we make for every
other course, or for tilkry one part per module.
We can skip a tilkry module the student didn't write about.
A module is needed if the assignment groups we found
(\cref{AssignmentGroups}) can match it---they do when the student wrote
about it, and also by default.
Should they match neither module, we fall back to the ordinary part, so
that we at least get the ordinary output.
<<functions>>=
summary_parts() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  if ! concerns_course "$file" tilkry; then
    echo ordinary
    return
  fi
  local groups=$(assignment_groups "$file") module found=""
  for module in ${TILKRY_MODULES}; do
    grep -qP "${groups}" <<< "${module}" && echo "${module}" && found=1
  done
  [[ -n "${found}" ]] || echo ordinary
}
@

The parts only differ in two options: which assignment groups ([[-A]])
and which summary module ([[-S]]).
A tilkry part summarizes its own module, only LAB1 with the tilkry
summary module.
[[results_command]] prints the head of the [[canvaslms results]] command
for a part, the missing and report commands add their own tails.
<<functions>>=
results_command() {
  local part="$1"
  local groups='"${ASSIGN_GRPS}"'
  local module=canvaslms.grades.conjunctavgsurvey
  if [[ "${part}" != ordinary ]]; then
    groups="${part}"
  fi
  if [[ "${part}" = LAB1 ]]; then
    module=canvaslms.grades.tilkryLAB1
  fi
  echo 'canvaslms results \'
  echo '  -c "${COURSE}" \'
  echo "  -A ${groups} \\"
  echo '  -u "${STUDENT}" \'
  echo "  -S ${module} \\"
}
@

The commands for the parts follow each other in the output, so we put a
blank line between them, just like between the commands of [[results]].
<<separate the command for [[part]] from the previous one>>=
[[ "${part}" != "${parts%%$'\n'*}" ]] && echo "echo"
@ We know the first part, so we only print the blank line before the
others.


\subsection{Generate the missing command}

We can use the [[canvaslms results --missing]] command to output any missing
assignments, one command per part.
<<construct [[missing]] command from email>>=
local part parts=$(summary_parts "$file")
for part in ${parts}; do
  <<separate the command for [[part]] from the previous one>>
  results_command "${part}"
  <<the [[--missing]] options for [[part]]>>
done
@

For the ordinary summary module, we want to change the default regex for
passing grades ([[-F]]) to include the numbers too (for the mandatory
surveys).
We also want to add a regex for the optional assignments ([[-O]]), which we
prefix with \enquote{Optional:}.
The tilkry module needs neither:
it knows the grades of LAB1 and never reports an optional lab missing.
<<the [[--missing]] options for [[part]]>>=
if [[ "${part}" = LAB1 ]]; then
  echo '  --missing'
else
  echo '  --missing -F "([A-EP]|complete|[0-9.]+%?)" \'
  echo '            -O "^Optional:"'
fi
@


\subsection{Generate the report command}

Then the reporting will be similar to the above, just without the
[[--missing]] flag.
<<construct [[report]] command from email>>=
local part parts=$(summary_parts "$file")
for part in ${parts}; do
  <<separate the command for [[part]] from the previous one>>
  results_command "${part}"
  <<rewrite the course code for [[ladok]]>>
done
@ We also want to rewrite the first column: the course code.
This is because we want to pipe the data to the [[ladok -v report -f]] command.
Usually the one in Canvas contains other data as well, like the semester.
We want to remove that and keep only the course code.
<<rewrite the course code for [[ladok]]>>=
echo '| sed -E "s/ ?[HV]T[0-9]{2}( \(.*\))?//" \'
@ Normally, that works.
However, sometimes it doesn't.
Some courses deviate, maybe due to being a manual merge of several course rooms
in Canvas.
So we add the following backup:
<<rewrite the course code for [[ladok]]>>=
echo '| awk -F "\t" -v OFS="\t" -v code=$COURSE_CODE "{\$1=code; print}"'
@

