\section{Carrying results over from earlier rounds}\label{CarryoverSection}

Every round of a course has some students who took it before.
They only do the assignments they haven't passed, and their earlier
results stay in the Canvas room of the round they took.
The commands of the results subcommands read one room
(\cref{GradingSchemes}): in the new one, [[--missing]] lists what such a
student passed a year ago, and the report never sees the module
complete.
So we copy the passed results into the new room, and since every course
we give has returning students, the course is an argument and not part
of the code.

This subcommand starts from a course and not from an email, but it
keeps the idea of the results subcommands: it prints commands for us to
look at, and we run them.
The commands here write grades to Canvas, which is all the more reason
to read them first.
\begin{minted}{bash}
edumail carryover tilkry27 > carryover.sh
less carryover.sh
bash carryover.sh
\end{minted}
What it does print, it must decide from what Canvas holds, so unlike the
results subcommands it reads Canvas itself.
It reads the new room and the earlier ones, works out which grades to
copy, and prints the commands:
<<functions>>=
carryover() {
  local course="$1" renamed="${2:-/dev/null}"
  <<find the room of [[course]] and its earlier rounds>>
  <<fetch the students, assignments and grades of the rounds>>
  carryover_plan "${data}" "${renamed}" "${id}"
  rm -r "${data}"
}
@ The second argument is a file of assignments that were renamed between
rounds; we come back to it in \cref{CarryoverRenamed}.
All the deciding is done by [[carryover_plan]], which reads only the
files that [[carryover]] fetched.
That way we can try the decisions on rounds that we make up
(\cref{CarryoverTesting}), without Canvas.
The files hold the students' grades, so we remove them when we are
done.


\subsection{The earlier rounds}

A course has a Canvas room for each round, and the rooms share nothing
reliable but the course code at the start of their Canvas course code:
the rooms of datintro include [[DD1301 HT20 (50850)]],
[[DD1301HT21]] and [[DD1301 HT23 (datintro23p2)]].
So we look up the room that [[course]] names and take its course code:
two letters and three digits, then a digit or, as in the
[[DA150X]] of the seed rules in the [[nymutt]] documentation, a letter.
We take it from the start of the Canvas course code and nothing more,
since [[DD1301HT21]] has no space after it.
<<variables>>=
KTH_COURSE_CODE="[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}[0-9A-Z]"
@ The grades will be written to the room that [[course]] matches, so it
must match exactly one.
We keep the room's Canvas ID, which the commands will name it by: a
regex on the name, such as [[tilkry27]], is easy to get wrong in a way
that matches another room too.
<<find the room of [[course]] and its earlier rounds>>=
local room=$(canvaslms courses -a -i "${course}")
if [[ -z "${room}" || $(wc -l <<< "${room}") -ne 1 ]]; then
  echo "${course}: must match exactly one Canvas room" >&2
  return 1
fi
local id=$(cut -f 1 <<< "${room}")
local code=$(cut -f 2 <<< "${room}" | grep -oE "^${KTH_COURSE_CODE}")
if [[ -z "${code}" ]]; then
  echo "${course}: no course code in $(cut -f 2 <<< "${room}")" >&2
  return 1
fi
@ Every other room with that code is an earlier round.
We don't compare dates:
some rooms have none (tilkry23 has no start date in Canvas), and a room
of a round that hasn't started holds no grades, so it costs nothing to
include.
Without the [[-a]], [[canvaslms courses]] lists only the current
rooms, and the earlier rounds are the ones that are over.
<<find the room of [[course]] and its earlier rounds>>=
local rounds=$(canvaslms courses -a -i \
               | awk -F '\t' -v code="${code}" -v id="${id}" \
                   'index($2, code) == 1 && $1 != id { print $1 }')
if [[ -z "${rounds}" ]]; then
  echo "# ${course}: no earlier rounds of ${code} in Canvas"
  return 0
fi
@ The guard for no rounds is not only for the message:
the regex we make of the rounds below would be [[^()$]], and we'd rather
not find out what Canvas makes of that.


\subsection{What the rounds hold}

From the new room we need its students, its assignments and which of
them are already graded; from the earlier rounds, every submission.
We keep each in a file of its own in a temporary directory, which is
all that [[carryover_plan]] gets.
<<fetch the students, assignments and grades of the rounds>>=
local data=$(mktemp -d)
canvaslms users -sc "^${id}$" | cut -f 2 > "${data}/students"
canvaslms assignments list -c "^${id}$" | cut -f 3 > "${data}/assignments"
canvaslms submissions list -c "^${id}$" -l \
  | awk -F '\t' '$4 != ""' | cut -f 2,3 > "${data}/graded"
canvaslms submissions list -c "^($(paste -sd '|' <<< "${rounds}"))$" -l \
  > "${data}/earlier"
cut -f 2 <<< "${room}" > "${data}/room"
@ A student is their login ID, the second column of [[canvaslms
users]] and, with [[-l]], the third of [[canvaslms submissions list]].
We don't go by the name, since two students can share one.
The other columns of [[submissions list]] are the room, the assignment,
the grade and the dates of submission and grading; a submission that
nobody has graded has an empty grade.
Canvas has a submission for every student and assignment, submitted or
not---tilkry25 has 87 students, 39 assignments and 3393
submissions---so the earlier rounds' file holds every student's grade in
every assignment of those rounds.
The rooms' names in its first column are what the comments to the
students will name.
Reading every submission of every earlier round takes a while:
for datintro26, with nine earlier rooms and some 21000 submissions, it
took six minutes.


\subsection{Deciding what to carry over}

[[carryover_plan]] reads the files and prints the commands, as one
[[awk]] program:
<<functions>>=
carryover_plan() {
  local data="$1" renamed="$2" id="$3"
  awk -F '\t' -v q="'" -v id="${id}" -v message="${CARRYOVER_MESSAGE}" \
      -v renamed="${renamed}" -v students="${data}/students" \
      -v assignments="${data}/assignments" -v graded="${data}/graded" \
      -v room="$(cat "${data}/room" 2>/dev/null)" '
    <<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>
    BEGIN { <<rank the passing grades>> }
    <<read the new room and the renamed assignments>>
    <<keep each student's best pass of each assignment>>
    END {
      <<print the commands that carry the grades over>>
      <<print what could not be carried over>>
    }' "${renamed}" "${data}/students" "${data}/assignments" \
       "${data}/graded" "${data}/earlier" \
  | sort -t $'\t' -k 1,1n -k 2,2nr -k 3 | cut -f 3-
}
<<variables>>=
CARRYOVER_MESSAGE="${CARRYOVER_MESSAGE:-Result carried over from}"
@ The program prints each line behind two numbers, which the [[sort]]
orders the lines by: what kind of line it is, the commands first and the
comments after, and then a count, the largest first; the lines that tie
on both are sorted as text.
The message is what the students see as a comment on the grade, with
the name of the round's room after it; it can be set from the
environment, for a course given in Swedish.

The files come in a fixed order, and we tell them apart by name.
Each of the rules below ends in [[next]], so only the last file, the
earlier rounds', reaches the rules after them.
<<read the new room and the renamed assignments>>=
FILENAME == renamed     { new_name[$1] = $2; next }
FILENAME == students    { student[$1] = 1; next }
FILENAME == assignments { in_room[$1] = 1; next }
FILENAME == graded      { done[$1, $2] = 1; next }
@ Counting files with [[FNR == 1]] would have been shorter, but an empty
file has no first line: a room where nothing is graded yet would have
been taken for the earlier rounds.

\subsubsection{Which grades count}

A grade is worth carrying over if it passes.
We count the grades that [[canvaslms]] counts as passing in its summary
modules: A to E, P, and the spellings \enquote{P+} and
\enquote{complete} that Canvas also uses for a pass.
Everything else stays: a fail, a grade in points, an empty grade.
Points are what datintro scores its interactive videos in, the group
\enquote{Learning material}, and that group belongs to no module.
Of several passes of the same assignment, from different rounds or a
redone one, we take the best: a letter above a pass, since it says
more.
\enquote{Complete} is just another way to write P, so the two are
equal.
<<rank the passing grades>>=
rank["A"] = 7; rank["B"] = 6; rank["C"] = 5; rank["D"] = 4; rank["E"] = 3
rank["P+"] = 2; rank["P"] = 1; rank["COMPLETE"] = 1
@ Between equal passes, we take the one graded last.
[[canvaslms]] prints the dates as [[YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm]], which sort as
strings, but it prints [[N/A]] when there is none, and that would sort
after every date; so we read it as no date.

\subsubsection{Renamed assignments}\label{CarryoverRenamed}

An assignment is the same in two rounds when it has the same name.
That is often not the case:
of the 48 assignments of tilkry26, 30 have a name from tilkry25.
Some were renamed and some are new, and only we know which is which.
So we may give a file of the renamed ones, each line the old name and
the new, separated by a tab, as for the rest of the data here:
\begin{minted}{text}
INL1Written	INL1Written Final
\end{minted}
The assignment groups (LAB1, INL1) did not change between the rounds,
but we don't carry whole groups over:
a group's assignments change too, and a student who passed the group a
year ago has the module in Ladok already.
What a returning student lacks are the assignments of a module they
did not finish.

\subsubsection{The best pass}

With that, the earlier rounds' file is read line by line.
<<keep each student's best pass of each assignment>>=
{
  <<skip what we shouldn't carry over>>
  key = name SUBSEP $3
  date = ($6 == "N/A" ? "" : $6)
  if (!(key in best) || r > best_rank[key] \
      || (r == best_rank[key] && date > best_date[key])) {
    best[key] = $4; best_rank[key] = r; best_date[key] = date
    from[key] = $1
  }
}
@ We skip the lines of students who are not in the new room, and the
grades that don't pass.
The assignment gets its new name, if it has one; if the new room has no
assignment by that name, we count the student and the grade under the
old name, to report it later.
What is graded in the new room already, we leave alone, even a fail:
whoever graded it saw something we don't.
<<skip what we shouldn't carry over>>=
if (!($3 in student)) next
r = rank[toupper($4)]
if (!r) next
name = ($2 in new_name ? new_name[$2] : $2)
if (!(name in in_room)) { lost[$2, $3] = 1; next }
if ((name, $3) in done) { kept[name, $3] = 1; next }
@

\subsubsection{The commands}

The students who get the same grade from the same round in the same
assignment can share a command, since [[canvaslms grade]] grades every
student its [[-u]] regex matches.
That makes far fewer calls to Canvas than one per student and
assignment.
<<print the commands that carry the grades over>>=
for (key in best) {
  split(key, part, SUBSEP)
  cmd = part[1] SUBSEP best[key] SUBSEP from[key]
  users[cmd] = (cmd in users ? users[cmd] "|" : "") regex(part[2])
}
for (cmd in users) {
  split(cmd, part, SUBSEP)
  printf "1\t0\tcanvaslms grade -c %s -a %s -g %s -m %s -u %s\n",
    quote("^" id "$"), quote("^" regex(part[1]) "$"), quote(part[2]),
    quote(message " " part[3] "."), quote("^(" users[cmd] ")$")
}
@ The names in the commands are regexes, and they are read by the shell
first.
An assignment name may hold anything---[[(]], a dot, an apostrophe---so
we escape what a regex would read as an operator, anchor it, and put it
in single quotes, which leave everything alone but a single quote.
That one we end the quote for, escape and start the quote again.
The program itself stands in single quotes in the script, so it can't
contain one; it gets it as the variable [[q]] instead.
<<[[awk]] functions for the carry-over>>=
function regex(s) {
  gsub(/[][\\.^$*+?(){}|\/]/, "\\\\&", s)
  return s
}
function quote(s) {
  gsub(q, q "\\" q q, s)
  return q s q
}
@

What we can't carry over, we report as comments, so that the output can
still go to the shell as it is.
A pass in an assignment that the new room doesn't have is what the file
of renamed assignments is for, so we list those names with the number
of students who passed them; the most common ones first, as they are the
ones most worth a line in that file.
<<print what could not be carried over>>=
for (key in lost) {
  split(key, part, SUBSEP)
  passed[part[1]]++
}
for (name in passed)
  printf "2\t%d\t# %d passed %s, which %s does not have\n",
    passed[name], passed[name], quote(name), room
for (key in kept) kept_count++
if (kept_count)
  printf "3\t0\t# %d left alone, as already graded in %s\n",
    kept_count, room
@


\subsection{Testing the decisions}\label{CarryoverTesting}

We make up two earlier rounds of tilkry and a new one, with a student
for each rule above:
\begin{description}
\item[returning] passed four assignments in tilkry25: one that is the
  same in the new room, one that was renamed, one whose name holds the
  characters that need escaping, and one that the new room doesn't have.
  In the quiz, the pass from tilkry26 is the later of two equal ones.
\item[again] took both rounds and passed the lab twice (P+, then P),
  the written assignment twice (A, then C) and the quiz twice, the
  second time without a grading date.
\item[second] passed the lab in tilkry25, like returning, so the two
  share a command.
\item[failed] failed the lab and got points in the quiz.
\item[graded] passed the lab, which is graded in the new room already.
\item[left] passed the lab but is not in the new room.
\end{description}
In the earlier rounds they have these grades, in the columns of
[[submissions list]] but without the submission date:
\begin{pycode}
