In the new room, graded's lab is graded already, and there is a file
renaming INL1Written to INL1Written Final.
With a Canvas ID for the new room, this is what we get:
\begin{pycode}
plan = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "carryover_plan", data,
                       os.path.join(data, "renamed"), "67592"],
                      capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
print(r"\begin{Verbatim}[breaklines,breakanywhere,fontsize=\footnotesize]")
print(plan, end="")
print(r"\end{Verbatim}")
\end{pycode}
Returning gets three grades from tilkry25 and the quiz from tilkry26;
the presentation is reported, as the new room doesn't have it.
Again gets P+ in the lab and A in the written assignment, the best of
two passes, and in the quiz the P of tilkry25: the other one has no
date.
Second shares returning's lab command.
Failed, graded and left get nothing, and graded's pass is counted as
left alone.

The output is meant for the shell, so let's give it to one.
In place of [[canvaslms]] we define a function that prints its
arguments, run each command line, and check the regexes as
[[canvaslms]] would read them, with Python's [[re]]:
the assignment's must match the name we meant and no other in the
room, and the users' must match exactly the students who get that
grade.
\begin{pycode}
import re, shlex
checks = []
for line in plan.splitlines():
  if line.startswith("#"):
    continue
  args = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c",
                         'canvaslms() { printf "%s\\n" "$@"; }; ' + line],
                        capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.splitlines()
  option = dict(zip(args[1::2], args[2::2]))
  names = [a for a in assignments if re.search(option["-a"], a)]
  users = sorted(s for s in students + ["left"]
                 if re.search(option["-u"], login(s)))
  checks.append(f"{names[0] if len(names) == 1 else names}: "
                f"{option['-g']} for {', '.join(users)}")
print_verbatim("\n".join(sorted(checks)))
\end{pycode}


\section{The HR subcommands}\label{HRSection}

Students are not the only ones who write to us about records.
Our teaching assistants are paid for the hours they have booked
themselves for in the sign-up sheets, and we keep those sheets with
[[nytid]].
Once a month [[nytid]] mails each assistant a draft time sheet: the
hours booked so far, which they report to KTH's HR system, Medvind, and
then confirm by answering the email.
We check what they reported against the bookings and generate the time
sheet.
Assistants on a contract instead (amanuenses) need the contract made,
and every assistant must be reported to HR as employed.
Each of these is one [[nytid hr]] command, and each wants to know which
assistant and which course rounds the email is about.

\begin{example}\label{email15}
An assistant confirms that they have reported their hours.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,69}]{text}{email15.txt}
We want to check what they reported and generate their time sheet, for
this assistant and the course rounds of the draft, with the time sheet
ending at the last session in it.
\end{example}

This is the same task as the results subcommands', so we solve it the
same way:
we print command lines into the reply, with the identifiers as
variables on top, to correct by hand where the email misled us.
\Cref{email15} should yield the following.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "hr", "email15.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(str(output.stdout, "utf-8"))
\end{pycode}
(The course rounds, the period and the validity depend on the day this
document is compiled and on the author's [[nytid]] registers; we will
see why below.)
Before this script, those lines were typed by hand, with the
assistant's username adapted each time---and that username is right
there in the address the email came from.

We print all the commands rather than guess which one the email asks
for.
The email in \cref{email15} could as well have been a question about
the contract, and a command we don't need is one line to skip, while
one we need but didn't get must be typed out after all.
The check of the report comes first, since it answers what such an email
raises: did they report the hours they should?
The time sheets are the detail to read when they didn't.
<<functions>>=
hr() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  shift
  medvind "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  timesheets "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  amanuensis "$file" "$@"
  echo "echo"
  employed "$file" "$@"
}
@

\subsection{The commands}

Each command gets a subcommand of its own, so that we can ask for just
one of them.
As with the results subcommands, whichever of them runs first prints
the variables.
<<functions>>=
medvind() {
  <<output HR variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[medvind]] command from email>>
}
timesheets() {
  <<output HR variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[timesheets]] commands from email>>
}
amanuensis() {
  <<output HR variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[amanuensis]] command from email>>
}
employed() {
  <<output HR variables if not already done>>
  <<construct [[employed]] command from email>>
}
@

The flag is not the one of [[<<output variables if not already done>>]]:
the HR variables are other variables, and if we call [[results]] and
[[hr]] in the same shell, each must still print its own.
<<output HR variables if not already done>>=
local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
if [[ -z "$HR_VARS_ALREADY_OUTPUT" ]]; then
  HR_VARS_ALREADY_OUTPUT=1
  <<echo the HR variables>>
fi
@

The time sheets come in two kinds, and [[nytid]] makes them in separate
runs:
with [[--no-amanuensis]], its default, it makes them for the hourly
paid assistants only, with [[--amanuensis]] for the amanuenses only,
whose hours beyond the contract are paid as \enquote{mertid}.
An assistant is one or the other, so one of the two commands yields
nothing; we spell out the default, so that the two lines show their
difference.
<<construct [[timesheets]] commands from email>>=
local kind
for kind in --no-amanuensis --amanuensis; do
  [[ "${kind}" = --amanuensis ]] && echo "echo"
  echo 'nytid hr timesheets generate "${COURSE}" \'
  echo '  --user "${TA}" --end "${END}" \'
  echo "  ${kind} \\"
  echo '  --no-store'
done
@ The [[--no-store]] makes it a preview, so nothing counts as paid until
we have looked at it.

The comparison with what the assistant reported we leave to [[nytid]],
which reads the reported hours from Medvind and prints nothing when they
match the bookings.
Unlike the time sheets, this takes one run for both kinds of assistants:
[[nytid]] subtracts what a contract covers from the bookings, which
leaves an amanuensis with the mertid only.
<<construct [[medvind]] command from email>>=
echo 'nytid hr medvind deviations "${COURSE}" \'
echo '  --user "${TA}" \'
echo '  --leader ".*" \'
echo '  #--to "${END}"'
@ Many assistants don't fill in the work leader when they report.
With the leader from our config, [[hr.medvind.leader]], those hours
would be left out and look like hours missing from the report.
So we compare the times reported to any leader, blank ones included.
That also counts what the assistant reported to someone else, so the
option has a line of its own, which is easy to delete when that matters.

We don't print the first date:
it is a setting of ours, [[hr.medvind.since]] in the config of [[nytid]].
We leave the last date to [[nytid]] too.
Assistants can report some hours, such as preparation, a few days after
the session, so the last session in the bookings needn't be the last date
reported in Medvind, and stopping there would miss those hours.
The default of [[nytid]] runs to the end of a month, picked by the cutoff
day, so it counts them when they fall in the same month.
Hours reported across a month's end show as a deviation until a later
month's check includes them.

That month is the draft's only up to the cutoff day of the next month.
A later check, or one from an email without a draft, also counts bookings
the assistant hasn't been asked to report, so a shortfall there needn't
be an error.
That is when we want to stop at the draft's last session:
[[--to]] stays as a comment for it, and removing the comment sign is all
it takes.
It must be the last line:
a comment ends the command, so any options below it would be lost.

The contract is made for a period, and the employment report is valid
until a date.
<<construct [[amanuensis]] command from email>>=
echo 'nytid hr amanuensis create --xlsx --xlsx-format TCS "${COURSE}" \'
echo '  --user "${TA}" \'
echo '  --period "${PERIOD}"'
<<construct [[employed]] command from email>>=
echo 'nytid hr employed report "${COURSE}" "${TA}" \'
echo '  --valid-until "${VALID_UNTIL}"'
@

What remains is to find the values of the variables:
the assistant ([[TA]]), the course rounds ([[COURSE]]), the draft's last
session ([[END]]), the period ([[PERIOD]]) and the validity
([[VALID_UNTIL]]).

\subsection{The assistant's username}

[[nytid]] knows the assistants by their KTH usernames, and the username
is the part before the @ of their KTH address.
When we reply, the assistant's address is in the [[To:]] line, as the
student's is for [[student_id]].
But the email we look at is not always our reply:
it may be the assistant's email as it arrived, and then they are in the
[[From:]] line and we are in the [[To:]] line.
So we take the first KTH address of those two lines that isn't our own.
Only the header lines count:
the quoted thread in \cref{email15} has [[From:]] and [[To:]] lines too,
but quoting puts a [[>]] in front of them.
<<functions>>=
ta_username() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local user
  for user in $(grep -E "^(To|From): " "$file" \
                | grep -Po "${KTH_USERNAME}"); do
    if [[ "${user}" != "${OUR_USERNAME}" ]]; then
      echo "${user}"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  return 1
}
<<echo the HR variables>>=
echo "TA=\"$(ta_username $file)\""
@ If there is no such address, the variable is printed empty, which is
easy to spot and fill in.

The username is [[USER_FORMAT]] in front of a KTH [[DOMAIN]], but it
must not be the tail of a longer local part.
The functional addresses have hyphens---[[hr-support@eecs.kth.se]] is
in the [[To:]] line of \cref{email13}---and without the lookbehind we
would take \enquote{support} for an assistant.
<<variables>>=
KTH_USERNAME="(?<![-.\w])${USER_FORMAT}(?=@${DOMAIN})"
@

Our own username we take to be our login name, as it is on the
author's machines; where it isn't, setting [[OUR_USERNAME]] in the
environment overrides it.
<<variables>>=
OUR_USERNAME="${OUR_USERNAME:-${USER}}"
@

\subsection{The course rounds}

The draft time sheet tells us which courses it was made for.
[[nytid]] writes the course regex it was given into the subject,
\enquote{preliminär tidrapport (datintro|prg[im]|tilkry)} in
\cref{email15}, and the assistant's answer keeps it.
But that regex is not what we typed by hand for this email:
it was [[(datintro|prgi)26]].
The regex has no year, so it matches the rounds of every year, and it
names courses that aren't running now---no prgm this year, and tilkry
not until the next period.
What we added by hand is our knowledge of which rounds we are giving
now.

[[nytid]] has that knowledge too.
Our own register lists only the rounds we are giving now:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["nytid", "courses", "ls"], capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
Those of them that the subject's regex matches are exactly the rounds we
meant, datintro26 and prgi26.
We output them as a disjunction, [[(datintro26|prgi26)]], which matches
the same courses as [[(datintro|prgi)26]].
This register is also the one the commands use, as none of them names
another, so the rounds we find are rounds the commands know.
<<functions>>=
hr_courses() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local regex=$(draft_course_regex "$file")
  [[ -n "${regex}" ]] || regex=$(course_candidates_regex "$file")
  [[ -n "${regex}" ]] || return 1
  local current=$(nytid courses ls 2>/dev/null | cut -f 2 \
                  | grep -E "${regex}" | sort)
  if [[ -n "${current}" ]]; then
    make_disjunct_regex <<< "${current}"
  else
    echo "${regex}${YEAR}"
  fi
}
<<echo the HR variables>>=
echo "COURSE=\"$(hr_courses $file)\""
@ When none of our current rounds match, we still have the courses, so
we guess the round of this year:
a draft for a round that is over is unlikely to be answered a year
later.
Piped, [[nytid courses ls]] prints one [[register<tab>course]] line per
course, hence the [[cut]]; the [[sort]] makes the output independent of
the order the register keeps.

The regex is the subject's if [[nytid]] wrote the subject.
<<functions>>=
draft_course_regex() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  grep -m 1 "^Subject: " "$file" | grep -Po "${DRAFT_SUBJECT}\K\S+"
}
<<variables>>=
DRAFT_SUBJECT="preliminär tidrapport "
@ Otherwise---say the assistant asks about their contract---we make a
regex of the courses the email names or suggests, the candidates of
\cref{CourseCandidates}.
The guard matters:
[[make_disjunct_regex]] makes [[()]] of no courses, and that would
match every course we have.
<<functions>>=
course_candidates_regex() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local nicks=$(course_candidates "$file")
  [[ -n "${nicks}" ]] && make_disjunct_regex <<< "${nicks}"
}
@

\subsection{The draft's last session}

The time sheet must end where the draft ended.
Sessions after it are in the sign-up sheet already, but the assistant
hasn't been asked to report them.
They will be in next month's draft; if they were also in this month's
time sheet, they would be reported as paid already, and the assistant
would never be asked to report them.
So [[END]] is the date of the last session in the draft.

The draft's table has a row per session, which starts with its date and
time; quoting and the mail client's line wrapping put [[>]] in front and
break the rows, but never before the time.
The time is what tells a row from any other line starting with a date.
Within the table the rows are in order, but we sort anyway, which costs
nothing and doesn't depend on [[nytid]]'s order.
<<functions>>=
reported_until() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  local last=$(grep -Po "${BOOKING_ROW_DATE}" "$file" | sort | tail -n 1)
  echo "${last:-$(date +%F)}"
}
<<variables>>=
BOOKING_ROW_DATE="^[>\s]*\K\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}(?=\s+\d{2}:\d{2}:\d{2})"
<<echo the HR variables>>=
echo "END=\"$(reported_until $file)\""
@ An email without a draft gets today's date, so that a time sheet made
from it contains only sessions that have taken place.
The deadline in the draft's text, \enquote{innan 2026-10-01}, is a date
too, but not at the start of a line nor followed by a time.

\subsection{The period and the validity}

The contract is made per period, and the periods are configured in
[[nytid]] with their dates:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["nytid", "hr", "periods", "list"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
We take the period we are in.
A request for a contract usually comes for the period we are in, or
just before the next one starts; in the latter case the variable is
the one to change.
ISO dates compare correctly as strings, so [[awk]] can do it.
<<functions>>=
current_period() {
  nytid hr periods list 2>/dev/null \
  | awk -v today="$(date +%F)" '$2 <= today && today <= $3 { print $1; exit }'
}
<<echo the HR variables>>=
echo "PERIOD=\"$(current_period)\""
@

An employment report holds until HR's records are reset, on the first
of July, and [[nytid]] wants the next such date.
It can compute it itself from the config key
[[hr.employment.reset_date]], but we print it rather than rely on that
key being set, since the line should work on any of our machines.
<<functions>>=
valid_until() {
  local year=$(date +%Y)
  if [[ "$(date +%m-%d)" < "${HR_RESET_DATE}" ]]; then
    echo "${year}-${HR_RESET_DATE}"
  else
    echo "$((year + 1))-${HR_RESET_DATE}"
  fi
}
<<variables>>=
HR_RESET_DATE="07-01"
<<echo the HR variables>>=
echo "VALID_UNTIL=\"$(valid_until)\""
@

\subsection{Testing on the emails}

The parts of \cref{email15} on their own:
the username, from the [[To:]] line; the course rounds, from the subject
and our register; and the last session, from the table.
\begin{pycode}
lines = []
for function in ["ta_username", "hr_courses", "reported_until"]:
    output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", function, "email15.txt"],
                            capture_output=True)
    lines.append(f"{function}: {output.stdout.decode().strip()}")
print_verbatim("\n".join(lines))
\end{pycode}
The contract thread's reminder (\cref{email13}) is an email we wrote
ourselves, to an assistant and to HR's functional address, and it has
no draft:
the assistant is the first address that is not ours, the course rounds
come from the course the thread names, and the end is today.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "hr", "email13.txt"],
                        capture_output=True)
print_verbatim(str(output.stdout, "utf-8"))
\end{pycode}


\section{Extracting time-tracking labels}\label{LabelsSection}

The same parsing that finds a course for the results subcommands can also
answer a different question: which time-tracking labels does this email
belong to?
We track our working time with [[nytid track]], which tracks any number of
concurrently running, free-form labels.
The [[nymutt]] integration (its own literate program,
[[nymutt.nw]], in this repository) starts labels when we open an email
in [[mutt]] and stops them when we return to the index.
It delegates the parsing to the [[labels]] subcommand, so that all
knowledge about what our emails look like stays in one place.

Where the results subcommands output regexes---they need to \emph{match}
course rounds like [[prgi23]]---time tracking needs stable \emph{names}.
A label is an identity that accumulates time across years, so we output
the course nick without any year suffix ([[tilkry]], not
[[tilkry(24|25|26)]]), and we strip the year from a nick the student
wrote ([[prgi23]] becomes [[prgi]]).
We also prefer the nick to the code even when the student wrote the code:
[[prgi]] rather than [[DD1317]], so that the time lands under the one
name we use for the course everywhere else in [[nytid]].
This is exactly what [[course_candidates]] outputs:
named courses as nicks, translated by [[nick_of_course_code]], or the
keyword families when nothing is named.
When only keywords identify the course we thus get both [[prgm]] and
[[prgi]]; the concurrent-label model makes that cheap, and the sum for
either course is still right.

A code the table doesn't know is different.
[[course_candidates]] passes it through when it is all the email names,
and for the results subcommands that is right: it is the best guess at
which course to look in.
As a label it is not.
A label accumulates time, so it should be a course we keep track of,
and a string that merely looks like a course code is easy to come by:
[[CF6064]] and [[UVZ138950]] are among the labels our time tracking
collected that way.
So the labels name only the courses in [[COURSE_CODES]].
An unknown code still does its other job, which is to keep us from
guessing (\cref{CourseCandidates}): a student who names someone
else's course has not written about ours, and gets no course label at
all.
If we do spend time on such a course, a user's rules file can name it,
see below.
<<functions>>=
known_courses() {
  local nick
  while read -r nick; do
    [[ -n "${nick}" && -n "${COURSE_CODES[$nick]}" ]] && echo "${nick}"
  done
  return 0
}
@ The test for an empty line comes first, since [[bash]] refuses an
empty subscript of an associative array.
Neither [[course_candidates]] nor [[named_courses]] prints an empty
line today, but both build their output from variables that may be
empty, and the test keeps such a slip from turning into an error
message for every email.

Assignment groups are labels too---[[INL1]] and [[LAB2]] are what we
already track by hand---so we reuse the search from [[assignment_groups]],
but not the default it falls back on:
a label must name something the email actually mentions.
But not every email about a course is about its modules.
\Cref{email5} is from someone who wants to \emph{be} a teaching
assistant; such an email, like a teaching assistant's own mail, concerns
the course as a whole.
The words in it still hit the module tests (\enquote{rättade
inlämningsuppgifter} suggests LAB1 for the datintro course) and the
examination rules (\enquote{rättade} again), and both would be the wrong
bucket.
So we test for that kind of email first, and derive the module-level
labels only when it isn't one.

One distinction comes before even that.
The keyword families and the module tests are guesses from words, made
for students who describe a course rather than name it---mail written
by people.
Mail written by programs uses the same words for other reasons.
\begin{example}\label{email9}
A cron job reports that the nightly backup failed.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,4,9}]{text}{email9.txt}
The word \enquote{ssh} is what a datintro student writes about, and
here it occurs three times.
\end{example}
Guessed from its words, \cref{email9} is a datintro email, module and
all; a GitHub notification fares no better, since \enquote{git} is in
its sender's name.
What a program \emph{states} is evidence all the same:
a script that reports grades names the course code and the assignment
group it reported.
So for such mail we keep what is stated literally and what the rules
say, and skip the guesses.
Which senders are programs is nothing this script can know---the list
changes with every tool we adopt---so the rules file says so, with a
[[stream:]] rule (\cref{LabelRules}).

One restriction applies to all of the above.
An email carries its thread along, quoted below what its author wrote,
and a thread drifts: what stood in it a year ago is not what we spend
time on today.
So before anything else we cut the email down to the message and the
one it answers (\cref{RecentPart}), and every test below reads only
that.
<<functions>>=
labels() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  <<narrow [[file]] to the message and the one it answers>>
  (
    if is_stream "$file"; then
      <<labels from what a stream states>>
    else
      course_candidates "$file" | known_courses
      if <<test for TA terms>>; then
        <<labels about the course as a whole>>
      else
        <<labels about the course's modules>>
      fi
    fi
  ) | sort -u
}
@ Duplicates are expected---a course both named and described by keywords,
or several rules yielding the same label---so we deduplicate, and the
sorted output doubles as a canonical form that callers can compare and
diff against (which [[nymutt]] does to keep timers running across
consecutive emails about the same course).

Emails about the modules get the assignment groups and everything the
user's rules say (\cref{LabelRules}).
<<labels about the course's modules>>=
<<find assignment groups in [[file]]>>
labels_rules "$file"
@

Emails about the course as a whole get the [[TA-management]] label
instead of assignment groups, and the rules' labels minus the
examination ones.
Those names are a convention of the seed rules that ship with
[[nymutt]] (the seed rules section of its documentation), which attach them to
module-level matters: grades, Ladok, resubmissions.
That is why this script may know them; a rules file that uses other
names for those matters must extend the list.
The user's own [[TA-management]] rules (timesheets, pay) must spell the
label as we do here, or the time splits over two labels.
<<labels about the course as a whole>>=
echo "TA-management"
labels_rules "$file" | grep -vxE "${EXAMINATION_LABELS}"
<<variables>>=
EXAMINATION_LABELS="examination|grading|ladok"
@

Mail from a program gets what it states and what the rules say.
The named courses are the ones [[course_candidates]] would have found;
what is left out is its fallback to the keyword families, and the
module tests, which guess from the same words.
The literal assignment groups stay, since a program that prints
[[LAB1]] means it.
There is no assistant test either: a program does not apply for a job.
<<labels from what a stream states>>=
named_courses "$file" | known_courses
<<literal assignment groups in [[file]]>>
labels_rules "$file"
@

\subsection{Recognizing mail about teaching assistants}

Three emails from one thread show what such mail looks like from our
side of it: we ask for a contract, the assistant answers, and the
personnel office answers.
\begin{example}\label{email6}
We ask the personnel office for an assistant's contract, as a
tab-separated line: identifier, name and address, first and last day,
percentage.
\inputminted[highlightlines={3,5,13,15}]{text}{email6.txt}
Nothing here names a course, and the only word about the employment is
the compound \enquote{amanuensavtal}.
\end{example}
\begin{example}\label{email7}
The assistant replies that the percentage is too high.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,4,11}]{text}{email7.txt}
The reply says \enquote{rättat till} about their bookings, which the
rules of \cref{LabelRules} would take for grading.
\end{example}
\begin{example}\label{email8}
The personnel office replies to us and a colleague, who both asked for
a contract with the same assistant, because the two add up to more
than the allowed 50\%.
\inputminted[highlightlines={2,5,12-14}]{text}{email8.txt}
The words are employment words---\enquote{timanställning},
\enquote{amanuenstjänsten}, \enquote{jobba på timme}---and the sender
is a support desk.
\end{example}
\begin{example}\label{email10}
The same matter from the other direction: a note to ourselves recording
who we have taken on.
\inputminted[highlightlines={4,12,16,18}]{text}{email10.txt}
This one offers no employment word at all.
It \emph{states} the employment instead, on the last line: a person, the
first and last day, and a share of full time---the line
\cref{email6} sends to the personnel office.
The only word for the people is the slang plural \enquote{assar}.
\end{example}
All three are about the assistant's employment and none about a module
of the course; which course it is the thread never says, so no course
can be derived from it.

The test is a conjunction:
the email must mention an assistant \emph{and} hint at employment.
A student writing \enquote{assistenten sa att det var godkänt} is asking
about a module, and the noun alone cannot tell that email from
\cref{email5}.
<<test for TA terms>>=
   <<look for>> "${TA_NOUN}" \
&& <<look for>> "${TA_EMPLOYMENT}"
@

The noun comes in Swedish inflections (\enquote{assistenten},
\enquote{assistenter}), in the compound \enquote{lärarassistent} that
applicants write, in the slang forms \enquote{asse}, \enquote{assar},
\enquote{assarna} and \enquote{assning}, and as \enquote{amanuens}, the
title of a student employed part time.
The short forms need word boundaries, or \enquote{asse} would match
inside far too many words.
The plural is spelled out as alternatives rather than left as a stem,
which would have been shorter:
\enquote{passar} and \enquote{klassar} end that way, and a stem also
matches surnames---\cref{email2} contains an \enquote{Assarssons}, and
that email is about a project, not about assistants.
\enquote{Amanuens} needs the opposite, a boundary in front only:
it is the head of the compounds that contract mail is written
in---\enquote{amanuensavtal}, \enquote{amanuensanställning},
\enquote{amanuenstjänst}---and \cref{email6} consists of little else.
No other Swedish word starts that way, so the bare stem is safe.
The abbreviation TA needs more:
we grep case-insensitively, but \enquote{ta} is a verb that opens half
of all Swedish subject lines, so that alternative opts out of the
case-insensitivity with PCRE's [[(?-i:...)]].
<<variables>>=
TA_NOUN="\b(assning|ass(e|ar|arna)|(lärar)?assistent(en|er|erna)?)\b"
TA_NOUN="${TA_NOUN}|\bamanuens|(?-i:\bTA\b)"
@

The employment hints are what \cref{email5} offers---\enquote{intresse
att vara lärarassistent}, \enquote{bli lärarassistent},
\enquote{praktiktimmar}, \enquote{varit lärarassistent}, \enquote{rollen
som lärarassistent}---and the obvious work words.
The verbs \enquote{bli}, \enquote{vara} and the word \enquote{som} are
everywhere, so they count only directly before the noun.
\enquote{Timmar} is deliberately not a hint:
students write about the hours they spent on a lab.
\enquote{Amanuens} is a hint by itself:
where \enquote{assistent} is what a student calls the person who graded
their lab, \enquote{amanuens} is a job title, so an email that uses it
is about the employment---\cref{email6} asks for a contract in a single
line with no other work word in it.
The word is therefore in both lists and carries the test alone.

An appointment can also be stated rather than described, and then there
is no word to find:
\cref{email10} says who, from when to when, and at what share of full
time, and nothing else.
That line is the last hint.
A percentage on its own would be too little---\cref{email3} is a student
asking about an \enquote{omdöme på 0\%} in Canvas---so the hint is the
whole shape: two dates and then a percentage.
Over the examples in this document it occurs in the four emails about an
appointment and nowhere else.
<<variables>>=
TA_EMPLOYMENT="\b(jobba|arbeta|anställ|ansök|praktik|intresse|rekryter"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}|arvode|lön\b)"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}|\b(bli|vara|varit|som)\s+(lärar)?assistent"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}|\bamanuens"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}|[0-9]{4}(-[0-9]{2}){2}\s+"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}[0-9]{4}(-[0-9]{2}){2}\s+[0-9]{1,3}\s*%"
@

The other form of employment, by the hour, has a word of its own, and
one more email from our side of such a thread shows what happens to it.
\begin{example}\label{email13}
We remind the personnel office of an assistant's contract, on top of
the thread so far.
\inputminted[highlightlines={4,16,26,33,40,43,46}]{text}{email13.txt}
We wrote three lines.
The subject says \enquote{timanställning} and the message we answer
\enquote{timanställd}; the only \enquote{amanuens} is a question two
messages down, and below that the thread is a year older and about
someone else's course.
\end{example}
Nothing in the lists above matches \enquote{timanställd}.
The stem \enquote{anställ} is there, but behind a word boundary, and
inside a compound there is none.
\Cref{email8} has the word too; there \enquote{amanuens} carried the
test, so nothing showed.
In \cref{email13} it also carried the test, from a question we asked in
passing, which is the right answer for the wrong reason: a thread
without that question would have been no assistant matter at all.

Dropping the boundary would not be enough, since the test wants a noun
as well and the email offers none within reach.
We treat the word like \enquote{amanuens} instead:
\enquote{timanställd} and \enquote{timarvoderad} name a form of
employment, no student writes them about a lab, so the stem goes in
both lists and carries the test alone.
Here the boundary in front is right, as both are the start of a word.
<<variables>>=
TA_HOURLY="\btim(anställ|arvoder)"
TA_NOUN="${TA_NOUN}|${TA_HOURLY}"
TA_EMPLOYMENT="${TA_EMPLOYMENT}|${TA_HOURLY}"
@

\subsection{How much of the thread to read}\label{RecentPart}

\Cref{email13} is three lines of message and forty of thread, and the
thread is where its labels used to come from.
Counting the [[>]] in front of a line gives its depth: what we wrote
is at depth zero, the message we answer at one, and so on down.
Read that way, the email says something different at every depth:
\begin{description}
\item[Depth 0] says that a contract is late, and nothing more.
\item[Depth 1] names the course, DD1317, and the form of employment.
\item[Depth 2] asks about \enquote{amanuens}.
\item[Depth 3 and below] is last year's thread: a colleague's course,
  DD1310, a programme code from its booking sheet, the colleague's name
  in an attribution line.
\end{description}
All of the last item became labels---another course, and with a rules
file (\cref{LabelRules}) a programme and a colleague---and none of it
is what the email is about.
Naming a course is deliberate, we said when we decided to read course
codes from the whole email; but it is deliberate for the one who wrote
it, in the message they wrote it in.

So [[labels]] reads a thread only as far as the message answered.
That one level we need.
A reply is about what it replies to:
our three lines carry no course and no employment word, the message
above them carries both, and a reply to a student's question should be
tracked like the question.
It is also where the student's disclaimer stands when we reply to a
student (\cref{StudentDisclaimer}).
Beyond that level each step adds less of the matter at hand and more
of where the thread came from.
<<functions>>=
recent() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  awk -v max="${QUOTE_DEPTH}" '{
    prefix = $0
    sub(/[^> ].*$/, "", prefix)
    if (gsub(/>/, "", prefix) <= max) print
  }' "$file"
}
<<variables>>=
QUOTE_DEPTH="${QUOTE_DEPTH:-1}"
@ The prefix is whatever precedes the first character that is neither
[[>]] nor a space, which covers both [[> >]] and [[>>]]; [[gsub]]
returns the number of [[>]] it removed from it, and that is the depth.
The headers are at depth zero, so the [[from:]] and [[subject:]] rules
still find them.
The depth can be set from the environment, like the rules file, to see
what a different cut would do---we do that in \cref{LabelsTesting}.

The cut is at one and not at two for a reason that \cref{email13} also
shows.
Mail clients re-wrap quoted text, and the tail of a wrapped line loses
the markers it had:
line 46 stands four messages down and counts as depth two.
A deeper cut lets such fragments in, and that one names DD1310.
The same weakness sets the limit of the method:
a client that quotes by indentation, or puts the older messages under a
\enquote{Original Message} header as in \cref{email8}, leaves the whole
thread at depth zero, and we read all of it as before.

As with the guessing, we rewrite [[file]] and leave the tests alone.
<<narrow [[file]] to the message and the one it answers>>=
local narrowed=$(mktemp)
recent "$file" > "${narrowed}"
file="${narrowed}"
@ Only [[labels]] does this.
The results subcommands look a student up, and for that the older
messages help: the student's identifier and the assignment may well
stand only in the first message of the thread.

\subsection{User-defined label rules}\label{LabelRules}

The course and assistant tests above change rarely, so hard-coding them
in this script is fine.
Label rules are different: a new mailing list, an administrative sender, a
committee---these appear all the time, and the person adding them is in
their mail client, not in this repository rebuilding the script.
So the remaining rules live in a configuration file, whose location
can be overridden from the environment---to try a rules file before
installing it, and for [[nymutt]]'s documentation to demonstrate its
seed rules without touching the author's own file,
<<variables>>=
DEFAULT_RULES="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/edumail/labels.rules"
LABELS_RULES="${LABELS_RULES:-${DEFAULT_RULES}}"
@ with one rule per line:
a pattern, whitespace, and the labels that pattern maps to.
A [[#]] starts a comment.
The pattern is a case-insensitive Perl regex, optionally prefixed by a
field to restrict where it must match:
[[from:]] matches only the \enquote{From: } header (this is how senders
become labels), [[subject:]] only the \enquote{Subject: } header, and no
prefix matches anywhere in the cleaned email.
A fourth prefix, [[stream:]], matches the \enquote{From: } header like
[[from:]] and says one thing more: the sender is a program.
For an email a [[stream:]] rule matches, [[labels]] skips its keyword
guesses and keeps only what the email states literally and what the
rules say (\cref{email9} shows why); such a rule may even carry no
labels, when that is all there is to say.
The prefix is deliberately not [[from:]] with a side effect:
a rule for a person is a [[from:]] rule, a rule for a tool is a
[[stream:]] rule, and the file shows which is which.
For example:
\begin{minted}{text}
# pattern                      labels
from:.*@ug\.kth\.se            admin
stream:Cron\sDaemon            admin
exjobb|thesis                  supervision exjobb
\end{minted}

We read the file with [[read]], which splits on whitespace:
the first word is the pattern, the rest are the labels.
This gives a trivially simple format at the price that a pattern cannot
contain literal whitespace---use [[\s]] in the regex instead.
Every matching rule contributes its labels; an email about two things gets
both sets, which is exactly what concurrent time tracking wants.
The rules answer two questions, though---which labels, and whether a
[[stream:]] rule matched---and both need the same walk through the
file.
Rather than two loops that each know the prefixes, one function reports
every matching rule as its field followed by its labels, and each
question is a thin reading of that report.
<<functions>>=
matching_rules() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  [[ -r "${LABELS_RULES}" ]] || return 0
  local pattern rule_labels field
  while read -r pattern rule_labels; do
    <<skip comments and blank lines>>
    <<split any [[field]] prefix off [[pattern]]>>
    <<report the rule if [[pattern]] matches [[field]]>>
  done < "${LABELS_RULES}"
}
@ A missing rules file is not an error:
the course tests alone are a useful default, and [[nymutt]] must keep
working on a machine where no rules have been written yet.

The labels are everything after the field, one per line as [[labels]]
prints them; [[cut -s]] drops a report line with no labels rather than
printing its field.
The stream question is answered by the field alone, matched as a whole
word so that a label-less [[stream]] line counts too.
<<functions>>=
labels_rules() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  matching_rules "$file" | cut -s -d' ' -f2- | tr ' ' '\n'
}
is_stream() {
  local file=$(file_or_stdin "$1")
  matching_rules "$file" | grep -qw '^stream'
}
@

A comment line's first word starts with [[#]], and on a blank line
[[read]] leaves the pattern empty.
<<skip comments and blank lines>>=
[[ -z "${pattern}" || "${pattern}" == \#* ]] && continue
@

The field prefix is split off with shell prefix removal rather than
parsed with a regex:
a colon is legal in a regex, so we only treat the three known prefixes
specially and let anything else---including a pattern that happens to
start with [[http:]]---fall through as a whole-email pattern.
<<split any [[field]] prefix off [[pattern]]>>=
field=any
case "${pattern}" in
  from:*)    field=from;    pattern="${pattern#from:}";;
  stream:*)  field=stream;  pattern="${pattern#stream:}";;
  subject:*) field=subject; pattern="${pattern#subject:}";;
esac
@

Matching selects the text to search and then reuses the same
case-insensitive Perl grep as the course tests.
The header fields are matched against the raw email, not the cleaned one:
[[clean]] exists to stop \emph{body} keywords from matching noise headers,
but a [[from:]] rule wants exactly a header line.
A [[stream:]] rule searches that same line; its extra meaning is read
off the field name by [[is_stream]], not acted on here.
The unquoted [[${rule_labels}]] in [[echo]] is deliberate:
word splitting collapses whatever whitespace separated the labels in
the file to single spaces, so the report has a fixed shape that [[cut]]
can take apart.
<<report the rule if [[pattern]] matches [[field]]>>=
case "${field}" in
  from|stream) grep -i "^From: "    "$file";;
  subject)     grep -i "^Subject: " "$file";;
  any)         clean "$file";;
esac | grep -qiP "${pattern}" \
  && echo "${field}" ${rule_labels}
@

\subsection{Testing on the emails}\label{LabelsTesting}

Let's run [[labels]] on the example emails.
(We point [[XDG_CONFIG_HOME]] at a nonexistent directory so the author's
personal rules file doesn't leak into this document.)
\Cref{email1} mentions the programming course by code (DD1317) and by
keywords, so the family collapses to the one course named:
\begin{pycode}
import os
norules = dict(os.environ, XDG_CONFIG_HOME="/nonexistent")
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email1.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email2} never names a course, but its subject
(\enquote{Projektgranskning}) hits the programming courses' keyword test,
so the email gets the whole family---and the project's assignment group:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email2.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email4} names the course in the body and the module in the subject,
so it gets just those two:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email4.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
\Cref{email5} is recognized as mail about a teaching assistant:
of the five courses named only ours remains, and no assignment group is
derived although the datintro keywords occur in the email:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email5.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The conjunction in the assistant test is what keeps a student's mention
of their assistant from being taken for assistant matters:
\begin{pycode}
email = "Subject: Re: LAB2\n\nAssistenten sa att det var godkänt.\n"
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=email.encode(),
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
That email is shorter than the ones students send, which end with the
mail system's disclaimer (\cref{StudentDisclaimer}).
Its \enquote{anställd} is an employment word, and it would complete the
conjunction for any student who mentions an assistant, if [[clean]] did
not drop the line.
The same email as it actually arrives:
\begin{pycode}
email += ("\nObservera att avsändaren inte är anställd vid "
          "Kungliga Tekniska högskolan.\n")
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=email.encode(),
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
Our own note about new assistants (\cref{email10}) needs both of the
additions above, the slang plural and the appointment line, and then
names the course the assistants are taken on for:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email10.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The conjunction still holds for the slang, which students use too:
without evidence of an appointment, \enquote{assarna} is a remark about
who grades, and the email stays a module matter.
\begin{pycode}
email = "Subject: LAB1\n\nAssarna har inte rättat min labb än.\n"
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=email.encode(),
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The contract thread (\cref{email6,email7,email8}) is recognized on the
word \enquote{amanuens} alone, and yields nothing else:
no course is named, no module mentioned, and without a rules file there
is nothing for the examination filter to remove.
\begin{pycode}
lines = []
for email in ["email6.txt", "email7.txt", "email8.txt"]:
    output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", email],
                            capture_output=True, env=norules)
    lines.append(f"{email}: " + " ".join(output.stdout.decode().split()))
print_verbatim("\n".join(lines))
\end{pycode}
What the rules add to the thread is shown with the seed rules in the
[[nymutt]] documentation.

The reminder about the hourly contract (\cref{email13}) is where the
cut in the thread decides the answer, so we run it at several:
the message alone, the default, one level too many, and the whole
thread as we used to read it.
\begin{pycode}
lines = []
for depth in ["0", "1", "2", "99"]:
    output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email13.txt"],
                            capture_output=True,
                            env=dict(norules, QUOTE_DEPTH=depth))
    lines.append(f"{depth:>2}: " + " ".join(output.stdout.decode().split()))
print_verbatim("\n".join(lines))
\end{pycode}
The message alone is an assistant matter on its subject, and the
message it answers adds the course.
One level further the re-wrapped line brings in a course that the
email is not about, and nothing at any depth adds what the default
lacks.

\Cref{email11} is what the guessing looked like before the sender
decided its reach: it used to name four courses and three of their
modules, on \enquote{sub\emph{script}ion},
\enquote{e\emph{spec}ially} and the \enquote{Computer Science} of the
school in the signature.
Now it names none, and without a rules file there is nothing left to
say about it:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email11.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout or b"(nothing)\n")
\end{pycode}
The first two of those hits were bad matching rather than bad reach:
\enquote{script} was inside \enquote{subscription} and \enquote{spec}
inside \enquote{especially}, so both keywords now require a word
boundary in front.
That matters for the emails we still read in full, the students'.
For \enquote{spec} the front was not enough, \cref{email12} showed, and
the stem may now only end as the project's specification
([[PROJECT_SPEC]]).
With that, and with its nick name read as a name
(\cref{CourseCandidates}), \cref{email12} is about datintro:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email12.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

\Cref{email9} shows the guesses at work on a program's output, and what
a [[stream:]] rule does about them.
With no rules, the cron job's \enquote{ssh} makes it a datintro email,
module and all:
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email9.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
A [[from:]] rule for the daemon adds its label and changes nothing else,
whereas the same rule as a [[stream:]] rule also takes the guesses away:
\begin{pycode}
import tempfile
lines = []
for prefix in ["from", "stream"]:
    rules = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", suffix=".rules", delete=False)
    rules.write(f"{prefix}:Cron\\sDaemon  admin\n")
    rules.close()
    output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email9.txt"],
                            capture_output=True,
                            env=dict(norules, LABELS_RULES=rules.name))
    lines.append(f"{prefix}: " + " ".join(output.stdout.decode().split()))
print_verbatim("\n".join(lines))
\end{pycode}

\Cref{email17}, our signed answer about the cron job, used to give
ten labels, nine of them from the signature.
With the signature dropped and only our modules and nick names read,
what is left is the module it names and the course its subject
suggests: [[restlabb]] is the programming courses' script.
\begin{pycode}
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels", "email17.txt"],
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
The guess is new.
Before, the email counted as naming courses---[[ht25]] and [[stud42]]
outside the signature, [[Kprgi24]] inside it---and a named course
turns the guesses off.

The patterns are the last defence, so let's see them alone.
We break the first armour line, so that the block isn't recognized,
and cut the signature's lines of 64 characters in two, so that they
are too short to be taken for an attachment's.
What is left of the signature is then just random text, and the only
thing in it the patterns accept is [[LAB9]], one of our modules
standing alone between two signs of base64.
Its [[zLAB4]] and [[Kprgi24]] are not accepted, since a letter stands
in front of them:
\begin{pycode}
signed = open("email17.txt").read()
lines = []
for line in signed.replace("-----BEGIN PGP", "BEGIN PGP").split("\n"):
    lines += [line[:40], line[40:]] if len(line) == 64 else [line]
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"],
                        input="\n".join(lines).encode(),
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

A signature that is cut short ends where the cut is.
Here we quote a student's signed message, trimmed below the armour's
first lines, and answer under the quote:
\begin{pycode}
email = ("Subject: Re: Inlämning\n\n"
         "> Här är min lösning.\n"
         "> -----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n"
         ">\n"
         "> iQIzBAABCgAdFiEE2vhBQThLHLiJRRDR8X4wAqLXj8gFAmr99HkACgkQ8X4wAqLX\n"
         "\n"
         "LAB3 är godkänd nu.\n")
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=email.encode(),
                        capture_output=True, env=norules)
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}
Had we dropped everything up to the last armour line, as a range in
[[sed]] would, the answer would have gone with the quote.

A student who names only a course that isn't ours gets no course
label, and no guess either: the code counts as named, so the
programming keywords (\enquote{labb 3}, \enquote{python}) are not
consulted.
Without the code, they are.
\begin{pycode}
email = ("Subject: Fråga om DD1320\n\nHur gör jag labb 3 i python?\n\n"
         "Observera att avsändaren inte är anställd vid "
         "Kungliga Tekniska högskolan.\n")
lines = []
for subject in ["Fråga om DD1320", "Fråga"]:
    text = email.replace("Fråga om DD1320", subject)
    output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=text.encode(),
                            capture_output=True, env=norules)
    lines.append(f"{subject}: " + " ".join(output.stdout.decode().split()))
print_verbatim("\n".join(lines))
\end{pycode}
A rules file that knows the course can label it all the same, as the
seed rules in the [[nymutt]] documentation do for DD1320.
Mail from a program is held to the same standard: a job that reports
on someone else's course states its code, and the code is no more a
label for that.
\begin{pycode}
rules = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", suffix=".rules", delete=False)
rules.write("stream:Cron\\sDaemon  admin\n")
rules.close()
email = ("From: Cron Daemon <root@example.org>\nSubject: Cron\n\n"
         "DD9999 LAB2: 3 rapporterade\n")
output = subprocess.run(["./edumail", "labels"], input=email.encode(),
                        capture_output=True,
                        env=dict(norules, LABELS_RULES=rules.name))
print_verbatim(output.stdout)
\end{pycode}

An email matching neither a course test nor a rule yields nothing at all;
it's the caller's job to fall back to something sensible---[[nymutt]]
always adds a base [[email]] label of its own.


